  Processing Data Dictionary (Energy+.idd) File -- Start
      1 !IDD_Version 9.4.0
      2 !IDD_BUILD 998c4b761e
      3 ! **************************************************************************
      4 ! This file is the Input Data Dictionary (IDD) for EnergyPlus.
      5 ! The IDD defines the syntax and data model for each type of input "Object."
      6 ! Lines in EnergyPlus input files (and IDD) are limited to 500 characters.
      7 !
      8 ! Object Description
      9 ! ------------------
     10 ! To define an object (a record with data), develop a key word that is unique
     11 ! Each data item to the object can be A (Alphanumeric string) or N (numeric)
     12 ! Number each A and N.  This will show how the data items will be put into the
     13 ! arrays that are passed to the Input Processor "Get" (GetObjectItem) routines.
     14 ! All alpha fields are limited to 100 characters.  Numeric fields should be
     15 ! valid numerics (can include such as 1.0E+05) and are placed into double
     16 ! precision variables.
     17 !
     18 ! NOTE: Even though a field may be optional, a comma representing that field
     19 !   must be included (unless it is the last field in the object).  Since the
     20 !   entire input is "field-oriented" and not "keyword-oriented", the EnergyPlus
     21 !   Input Processor must have some representation (even if blank) for each
     22 !   field.
     23 !
     24 ! Object Documentation
     25 ! --------------------
     26 ! In addition, the following special comments appear one per line and
     27 ! most are followed by a value.  Comments may apply to a field or the object
     28 ! or a group of objects.
     29 !
     30 ! Field-level comments:
     31 !
     32 !  \field           Name of field
     33 !                     (should be succinct and readable, blanks are encouraged)
     34 !
     35 !  \note            Note describing the field and its valid values. If multiple lines,
     36 !                   start each line with \note. Limit line length to 100 characters.
     37 !
     38 !  \required-field  To flag fields which must have a value. If the idf input is blank and
     39 !                   there is a \default, then the default will be used. However, as of v8.6.0
     40 !                   the use of \required-field and \default on the same field is discouraged
     41 !                   and instances with both have been changed.
     42 !                     (this comment has no "value")
     43 !
     44 !  \begin-extensible  Marks the first field at which the object accepts an extensible
     45 !                   field set.  A fixed number of fields from this marker define the
     46 !                   extensible field set, see the object code \extensible for
     47 !                   more information.
     48 !
     49 !  \units           Units (must be from EnergyPlus standard units list)
     50 !                   EnergyPlus units are standard SI units
     51 !
     52 !  \ip-units        IP-Units (for use by input processors with IP units)
     53 !                   This is only used if the default conversion is not
     54 !                   appropriate.
     55 !
     56 !  \unitsBasedOnField  For fields that may have multiple possible units, indicates
     57 !                   the field in the object that can be used to determine
     58 !                   the units. The field reference is in the A2 form.
     59 !
     60 !  \minimum         Minimum that includes the following value
     61 !
     62 !  \minimum>        Minimum that must be > than the following value
     63 !
     64 !  \maximum         Maximum that includes the following value
     65 !
     66 !  \maximum<        Maximum that must be < than the following value
     67 !
     68 !  \default         Default for the field (if N/A then omit entire line). If a default is
     69 !                   added to an existing field, then \required-field should be removed if present.
     70 !                   Defaults are filled in only if the field is within \min-fields, or the actual
     71 !                   object is longer than this field.
     72 !
     73 !  \deprecated      This field is not really used and will be deleted from the object.
     74 !                   The required information is gotten internally or
     75 !                   not needed by the program.
     76 !
     77 !  \autosizable     Flag to indicate that this field can be used with the Auto
     78 !                   Sizing routines to produce calculated results for the
     79 !                   field.  If a value follows this, then that will be used
     80 !                   when the "Autosize" feature is flagged.  To trigger
     81 !                   autosizing for a field, enter Autosize as the field's
     82 !                   value.  Only applicable to numeric fields.
     83 !
     84 !  \autocalculatable Flag to indicate that this field can be automatically
     85 !                   calculated. To trigger auto calculation for a field, enter
     86 !                   Autocalculate as the field's value.  Only applicable to
     87 !                   numeric fields.
     88 !
     89 !  \type            Type of data for the field -
     90 !                     integer
     91 !                     real
     92 !                     alpha       (arbitrary string),
     93 !                     choice      (alpha with specific list of choices, see
     94 !                                 \key)
     95 !                     object-list (link to a list of objects defined elsewhere,
     96 !                                  see \object-list and \reference)
     97 !                     external-list (uses a special list from an external source,
     98 !                                    see \external-list)
     99 !                     node        (name used in connecting HVAC components)
    100 !
    101 !  \retaincase      Retains the alphabetic case for alpha type fields
    102 !
    103 !  \key             Possible value for "\type choice" (blanks are significant)
    104 !                     use multiple \key lines to indicate all valid choices
    105 !
    106 !  \object-list     Name of a list of user-provided object names that are valid
    107 !                     entries for this field (used with "\reference")
    108 !                     see Zone and BuildingSurface:Detailed objects below for
    109 !                     examples.
    110 !                  ** Note that a field may have multiple \object-list commands.
    111 !
    112 !  \external-list   The values for this field should be selected from a special
    113 !                   list generated outside of the IDD file. The choices for the
    114 !                   special lists are:
    115 !                        autoRDDvariable
    116 !                        autoRDDmeter
    117 !                        autoRDDvariableMeter
    118 !                   When one of these are selected the options for the field
    119 !                   are taken from the RDD or MDD file or both.
    120 !
    121 !  \reference       Name of a list of names to which this object belongs
    122 !                     used with "\type object-list" and with "\object-list"
    123 !                     see Zone and BuildingSurface:Detailed objects below for
    124 !                     examples:
    125 !
    126 !                        Zone,
    127 !                          A1 , \field Name
    128 !                               \type alpha
    129 !                               \reference ZoneNames
    130 !
    131 !                        BuildingSurface:Detailed,
    132 !                          A4 , \field Zone Name
    133 !                               \note Zone the surface is a part of
    134 !                               \type object-list
    135 !                               \object-list ZoneNames
    136 !
    137 !             For each zone, the field "Name" may be referenced
    138 !             by other objects, such as BuildingSurface:Detailed, so it is
    139 !             commented with "\reference ZoneNames"
    140 !             Fields that reference a zone name, such as BuildingSurface:Detailed's
    141 !             "Zone Name", are commented as
    142 !             "\type object-list" and "\object-list ZoneNames"
    143 !             ** Note that a field may have multiple \reference commands.
    144 !             ** This is useful if the object belongs to a small specific
    145 !             object-list as well as a larger more general object-list.
    146 !
    147 ! Object-level comments:
    148 !
    149 !  \memo            Memo describing the object. If multiple lines, start each line
    150 !                   with \memo.
    151 !                   Limit line length to 100 characters.
    152 !
    153 !  \unique-object   To flag objects which should appear only once in an idf
    154 !                     (this comment has no "value")
    155 !
    156 !  \required-object To flag objects which are required in every idf
    157 !                     (this comment has no "value")
    158 !
    159 !  \min-fields      Minimum number of fields that should be included in the
    160 !                   object.  If appropriate, the Input Processor will fill
    161 !                   any missing fields with defaults (for numeric fields).
    162 !                   It will also supply that number of fields to the "get"
    163 !                   routines using blanks for alpha fields (note -- blanks
    164 !                   may not be allowable for some alpha fields).
    165 !
    166 !  \obsolete        This object has been replaced though is kept (and is read)
    167 !                   in the current version.  Please refer to documentation as
    168 !                   to the dispersal of the object.  If this object is
    169 !                   encountered in an IDF, the InputProcessor will post an
    170 !                   appropriate message to the error file.
    171 !                   usage:  \obsolete New=>[New object name]
    172 !
    173 !  \extensible:<#>  This object is dynamically extensible -- meaning, if you
    174 !           change the IDD appropriately (if the object has a simple list
    175 !           structure -- just add items to the list arguments (i.e. BRANCH
    176 !           LIST). These will be automatically redimensioned and used during
    177 !           the simulation. <#> should be entered by the developer to signify
    178 !           how many of the last fields are needed to be extended (and EnergyPlus
    179 !           will attempt to auto-extend the object).  The first field of the first
    180 !           instance of the extensible field set is marked with \begin-extensible.
    181 !
    182 !  \begin-extensible See previous item, marks beginning of extensible fields in
    183 !                   an object.
    184 !
    185 !  \format          The object should have a special format when saved in
    186 !                   the IDF Editor with the special format option enabled.
    187 !                   The options include SingleLine, Vertices, CompactSchedule,
    188 !                   FluidProperties, ViewFactors, and Spectral.
    189 !                   The SingleLine option puts all the fields for the object
    190 !                   on a single line. The Vertices option is used in objects
    191 !                   that use X, Y and Z fields to format those three fields
    192 !                   on a single line.
    193 !                   The CompactSchedule formats that specific object.
    194 !                   The FluidProperty option formats long lists of fluid
    195 !                   properties to ten values per line.
    196 !                   The ViewFactor option formats three fields related to
    197 !                   view factors per line.
    198 !                   The Spectral option formats the four fields related to
    199 !                   window glass spectral data per line.
    200 !
    201 !   \reference-class-name Adds the name of the class to the reference list
    202 !                   similar to \reference.
    203 !
    204 ! Group-level comments:
    205 !
    206 !  \group          Name for a group of related objects
    207 !
    208 !
    209 ! Notes on comments
    210 ! -----------------
    211 !
    212 ! 1.  If a particular comment is not applicable (such as units, or default)
    213 ! then simply omit the comment rather than indicating N/A.
    214 !
    215 ! 2.  Memos and notes should be brief (recommend 5 lines or less per block).
    216 ! More extensive explanations are expected to be in the user documentation
    217 !
    218 ! Default IP conversions (no \ip-units necessary)
    219 !      $/(m3/s)               =>   $/(ft3/min)         0.000472000059660808
    220 !      $/(W/K)                =>   $/(Btu/h-F)         0.52667614683731
    221 !      $/kW                   =>   $/(kBtuh/h)         0.293083235638921
    222 !      $/m2                   =>   $/ft2               0.0928939733269818
    223 !      $/m3                   =>   $/ft3               0.0283127014102352
    224 !      (kg/s)/W               =>   (lbm/sec)/(Btu/hr)  0.646078115385742
    225 !      1/K                    =>   1/F                 0.555555555555556
    226 !      1/m                    =>   1/ft                0.3048
    227 !      A/K                    =>   A/F                 0.555555555555556
    228 !      C                      =>   F                   1.8 (plus 32)
    229 !      cm                     =>   in                  0.3937
    230 !      cm2                    =>   inch2               0.15500031000062
    231 !      deltaC                 =>   deltaF              1.8
    232 !      deltaC/hr              =>   deltaF/hr           1.8
    233 !      deltaJ/kg              =>   deltaBtu/lb         0.0004299
    234 !      g/GJ                   =>   lb/MWh              0.00793664091373665
    235 !      g/kg                   =>   grains/lb           7
    236 !      g/MJ                   =>   lb/MWh              7.93664091373665
    237 !      g/mol                  =>   lb/mol              0.0022046
    238 !      g/m-s                  =>   lb/ft-s             0.000671968949659
    239 !      g/m-s-K                =>   lb/ft-s-F           0.000373574867724868
    240 !      GJ                     =>   ton-hrs             78.9889415481832
    241 !      J                      =>   Wh                  0.000277777777777778
    242 !      J/K                    =>   Btu/F               526.565
    243 !      J/kg                   =>   Btu/lb              0.00042986 (plus 7.686)
    244 !      J/kg-K                 =>   Btu/lb-F            0.000239005736137667
    245 !      J/kg-K2                =>   Btu/lb-F2           0.000132889924714692
    246 !      J/kg-K3                =>   Btu/lb-F3           7.38277359526066E-05
    247 !      J/m2-K                 =>   Btu/ft2-F           4.89224766847393E-05
    248 !      J/m3                   =>   Btu/ft3             2.68096514745308E-05
    249 !      J/m3-K                 =>   Btu/ft3-F           1.49237004739337E-05
    250 !      K                      =>   R                   1.8
    251 !      K/m                    =>   F/ft                0.54861322767449
    252 !      kg                     =>   lb                  2.2046
    253 !      kg/J                   =>   lb/Btu              2325.83774250441
    254 !      kg/kg-K                =>   lb/lb-F             0.555555555555556
    255 !      kg/m                   =>   lb/ft               0.67196893069637
    256 !      kg/m2                  =>   lb/ft2              0.204794053596664
    257 !      kg/m3                  =>   lb/ft3              0.062428
    258 !      kg/m-s                 =>   lb/ft-s             0.67196893069637
    259 !      kg/m-s-K               =>   lb/ft-s-F           0.373316072609094
    260 !      kg/m-s-K2              =>   lb/ft-s-F2          0.207397818116164
    261 !      kg/Pa-s-m2             =>   lb/psi-s-ft2        1412.00523459398
