    379 
    380 \group Simulation Parameters
    381 
    382 Version,
    383       \memo Specifies the EnergyPlus version of the IDF file.
    384       \unique-object
    385       \format singleLine
    386   A1 ; \field Version Identifier
    387       \default 9.4
    388 
    389 SimulationControl,
    390       \unique-object
    391       \memo Note that the following 3 fields are related to the Sizing:Zone, Sizing:System,
    392       \memo and Sizing:Plant objects.  Having these fields set to Yes but no corresponding
    393       \memo Sizing object will not cause the sizing to be done. However, having any of these
    394       \memo fields set to No, the corresponding Sizing object is ignored.
    395       \memo Note also, if you want to do system sizing, you must also do zone sizing in the same
    396       \memo run or an error will result.
    397       \min-fields 7
    398   A1, \field Do Zone Sizing Calculation
    399       \note If Yes, Zone sizing is accomplished from corresponding Sizing:Zone objects
    400       \note and autosize fields.
    401       \type choice
    402       \key Yes
    403       \key No
    404       \default No
    405   A2, \field Do System Sizing Calculation
    406       \note If Yes, System sizing is accomplished from corresponding Sizing:System objects
    407       \note and autosize fields.
    408       \note If Yes, Zone sizing (previous field) must also be Yes.
    409       \type choice
    410       \key Yes
    411       \key No
    412       \default No
    413   A3, \field Do Plant Sizing Calculation
    414       \note If Yes, Plant sizing is accomplished from corresponding Sizing:Plant objects
    415       \note and autosize fields.
    416       \type choice
    417       \key Yes
    418       \key No
    419       \default No
    420   A4, \field Run Simulation for Sizing Periods
    421       \note If Yes, SizingPeriod:* objects are executed and results from those may be displayed..
    422       \type choice
    423       \key Yes
    424       \key No
    425       \default Yes
    426   A5, \field Run Simulation for Weather File Run Periods
    427       \note If Yes, RunPeriod:* objects are executed and results from those may be displayed..
    428       \type choice
    429       \key Yes
    430       \key No
    431       \default Yes
    432   A6, \field Do HVAC Sizing Simulation for Sizing Periods
    433       \note If Yes, SizingPeriod:* objects are exectuted additional times for advanced sizing.
    434       \note Currently limited to use with coincident plant sizing, see Sizing:Plant object
    435       \type choice
    436       \key Yes
    437       \key No
    438       \default No
    439   N1; \field Maximum Number of HVAC Sizing Simulation Passes
    440       \note the entire set of SizingPeriod:* objects may be repeated to fine tune size results
    441       \note this input sets a limit on the number of passes that the sizing algorithms can repeate the set
    442       \type integer
    443       \minimum 1
    444       \default 1
    445 
    446 PerformancePrecisionTradeoffs,
    447       \unique-object
    448       \memo This object enables users to choose certain options that speed up EnergyPlus simulation,
    449       \memo but may lead to small decreases in accuracy of results.
    450   A1, \field Use Coil Direct Solutions
    451       \note If Yes, an analytical or empirical solution will be used to replace iterations in
    452       \note the coil performance calculations.
    453       \type choice
    454       \key Yes
    455       \key No
    456       \default No
    457   A2, \field Zone Radiant Exchange Algorithm
    458       \note Determines which algorithm will be used to solve long wave radiant exchange among surfaces within a zone.
    459       \type choice
    460       \key ScriptF
    461       \key CarrollMRT
    462       \default ScriptF
    463   A3, \field Override Mode
    464       \note The increasing mode number roughly correspond with increased speed. A description of each mode
    465       \note are shown in the documentation. When Advanced is selected the N1 field value is used.
    466       \type choice
    467       \key Normal
    468       \key Mode01
    469    \key Mode02
      ***** Tabs eliminated from above line
    470       \key Mode03
    471       \key Mode04
    472       \key Mode05
    473       \key Mode06
    474    \key Mode07
      ***** Tabs eliminated from above line
    475       \key Advanced
    476       \default Normal
    477   N1, \field MaxZoneTempDiff
    478       \note Maximum zone temperature change before HVAC timestep is shortened.
    479       \note Only used when Override Mode is set to Advanced
    480       \type real
    481       \minimum 0.1
    482       \maximum 3.0
    483       \default 0.3
    484   N2; \field MaxAllowedDelTemp
    485       \note Maximum surface temperature change before HVAC timestep is shortened.
    486       \note Only used when Override Mode is set to Advanced
    487       \type real
    488       \minimum 0.002
    489       \maximum 0.1
    490       \default 0.002
    491 
    492 Building,
    493        \memo Describes parameters that are used during the simulation
    494        \memo of the building. There are necessary correlations between the entries for
    495        \memo this object and some entries in the Site:WeatherStation and
    496        \memo Site:HeightVariation objects, specifically the Terrain field.
    497        \unique-object
    498        \required-object
    499        \min-fields 8
    500   A1 , \field Name
    501        \retaincase
    502        \default NONE
    503   N1 , \field North Axis
    504        \note degrees from true North
    505        \units deg
    506        \type real
    507        \default 0.0
    508   A2 , \field Terrain
    509        \note  Country=FlatOpenCountry | Suburbs=CountryTownsSuburbs | City=CityCenter | Ocean=body of water (5km) | Urban=Urban-Industrial-Forest
    510        \type choice
    511        \key Country
    512        \key Suburbs
    513        \key City
    514        \key Ocean
    515        \key Urban
    516        \default Suburbs
    517   N2 , \field Loads Convergence Tolerance Value
    518        \note Loads Convergence Tolerance Value is a change in load from one warmup day to the next
    519        \type real
    520        \minimum> 0.0
    521        \maximum .5
    522        \default .04
    523        \units W
    524   N3 , \field Temperature Convergence Tolerance Value
    525        \units deltaC
    526        \type real
    527        \minimum> 0.0
    528        \maximum .5
    529        \default .4
    530   A3 , \field Solar Distribution
    531        \note  MinimalShadowing | FullExterior | FullInteriorAndExterior | FullExteriorWithReflections | FullInteriorAndExteriorWithReflections
    532        \type choice
    533        \key MinimalShadowing
    534        \key FullExterior
    535        \key FullInteriorAndExterior
    536        \key FullExteriorWithReflections
    537        \key FullInteriorAndExteriorWithReflections
    538        \default FullExterior
    539   N4 , \field Maximum Number of Warmup Days
    540        \note EnergyPlus will only use as many warmup days as needed to reach convergence tolerance.
    541        \note This field's value should NOT be set less than 25.
    542        \type integer
    543        \minimum> 0
    544        \default 25
    545   N5 ; \field Minimum Number of Warmup Days
    546        \note The minimum number of warmup days that produce enough temperature and flux history
    547        \note to start EnergyPlus simulation for all reference buildings was suggested to be 6.
    548        \note However this can lead to excessive run times as warmup days can be repeated needlessly.
    549        \note For faster execution rely on the convergence criteria to detect when warmup is complete.
    550        \note When this field is greater than the maximum warmup days defined previous field
    551        \note the maximum number of warmup days will be reset to the minimum value entered here.
    552        \note Warmup days will be set to be the value you entered. The default is 1.
    553        \type integer
    554        \minimum> 0
    555        \default 1
    556 
    557 ShadowCalculation,
    558        \unique-object
    559        \memo This object is used to control details of the solar, shading, and daylighting models
    560        \extensible:1
    561   A1 , \field Shading Calculation Method
    562        \note Select between CPU-based polygon clipping method, the GPU-based pixel counting method,
    563        \note or importing from external shading data.
    564        \note If PixelCounting is selected and GPU hardware (or GPU emulation) is not available, a warning will be
    565        \note displayed and EnergyPlus will revert to PolygonClipping.
    566        \note If Scheduled is chosen, the External Shading Fraction Schedule Name is required
    567        \note in SurfaceProperty:LocalEnvironment.
    568        \note If Imported is chosen, the Schedule:File:Shading object is required.
    569        \type choice
    570        \key PolygonClipping
    571        \key PixelCounting
    572        \key Scheduled
    573        \key Imported
    574        \default PolygonClipping
    575   A2 , \field Shading Calculation Update Frequency Method
    576        \note choose calculation frequency method. note that Timestep is only needed for certain cases
    577        \note and can increase execution time significantly.
    578        \type choice
    579        \key Periodic
    580        \key Timestep
    581        \default Periodic
    582   N1 , \field Shading Calculation Update Frequency
    583        \type integer
    584        \minimum 1
    585        \default 20
    586        \note enter number of days
    587        \note this field is only used if the previous field is set to Periodic
    588        \note warning issued if >31
    589   N2 , \field Maximum Figures in Shadow Overlap Calculations
    590        \note Number of allowable figures in shadow overlap in PolygonClipping calculations
    591        \type integer
    592        \minimum 200
    593        \default 15000
    594   A3 , \field Polygon Clipping Algorithm
    595        \note Advanced Feature.  Internal default is SutherlandHodgman
    596        \note Refer to InputOutput Reference and Engineering Reference for more information
    597        \type choice
    598        \key ConvexWeilerAtherton
    599        \key SutherlandHodgman
    600        \key SlaterBarskyandSutherlandHodgman
    601        \default SutherlandHodgman
    602   N3 , \field Pixel Counting Resolution
    603        \note Number of pixels in both dimensions of the surface rendering
    604        \type integer
    605        \default 512
    606   A4 , \field Sky Diffuse Modeling Algorithm
    607        \note Advanced Feature.  Internal default is SimpleSkyDiffuseModeling
    608        \note If you have shading elements that change transmittance over the
    609        \note year, you may wish to choose the detailed method.
    610        \note Refer to InputOutput Reference and Engineering Reference for more information
    611        \type choice
    612        \key SimpleSkyDiffuseModeling
    613        \key DetailedSkyDiffuseModeling
    614        \default SimpleSkyDiffuseModeling
    615   A5 , \field Output External Shading Calculation Results
    616        \type choice
    617        \key Yes
    618        \key No
    619        \default No
    620        \note If Yes is chosen, the calculated external shading fraction results will be saved to an external CSV file with surface names as the column headers.
    621   A6 , \field Disable Self-Shading Within Shading Zone Groups
    622        \note If Yes, self-shading will be disabled from all exterior surfaces in a given Shading Zone Group to surfaces within
    623        \note the same Shading Zone Group.
    624        \note If both Disable Self-Shading Within Shading Zone Groups and Disable Self-Shading From Shading Zone Groups to Other Zones = Yes,
    625        \note then all self-shading from exterior surfaces will be disabled.
    626        \note If only one of these fields = Yes, then at least one Shading Zone Group must be specified, or this field will be ignored.
    627        \note Shading from Shading:* surfaces, overhangs, fins, and reveals will not be disabled.
    628        \type choice
    629        \key Yes
    630        \key No
    631        \default No
    632   A7 , \field Disable Self-Shading From Shading Zone Groups to Other Zones
    633        \note If Yes, self-shading will be disabled from all exterior surfaces in a given Shading Zone Group to all other zones in the model.
    634        \note If both Disable Self-Shading Within Shading Zone Groups and Disable Self-Shading From Shading Zone Groups to Other Zones = Yes,
    635        \note then all self-shading from exterior surfaces will be disabled.
    636        \note If only one of these fields = Yes, then at least one Shading Zone Group must be specified, or this field will be ignored.
    637        \note Shading from Shading:* surfaces, overhangs, fins, and reveals will not be disabled.
    638        \type choice
    639        \key Yes
    640        \key No
    641        \default No
    642   A8 , \field Shading Zone Group 1 ZoneList Name
    643        \note Specifies a group of zones which are controlled by the Disable Self-Shading fields.
    644        \type object-list
    645        \object-list ZoneListNames
    646        \begin-extensible
    647   A9 , \field Shading Zone Group 2 ZoneList Name
    648        \type object-list
    649        \object-list ZoneListNames
    650   A10, \field Shading Zone Group 3 ZoneList Name
    651        \type object-list
    652        \object-list ZoneListNames
    653   A11, \field Shading Zone Group 4 ZoneList Name
    654        \type object-list
    655        \object-list ZoneListNames
    656   A12, \field Shading Zone Group 5 ZoneList Name
    657        \type object-list
    658        \object-list ZoneListNames
    659   A13; \field Shading Zone Group 6 ZoneList Name
    660        \type object-list
    661        \object-list ZoneListNames
    662 
    663 SurfaceConvectionAlgorithm:Inside,
    664        \memo Default indoor surface heat transfer convection algorithm to be used for all zones
    665        \unique-object
    666        \format singleLine
    667   A1 ; \field Algorithm
    668        \type choice
    669        \key Simple
    670        \key TARP
    671        \key CeilingDiffuser
    672        \key AdaptiveConvectionAlgorithm
    673        \default TARP
    674        \note Simple = constant value natural convection (ASHRAE)
    675        \note TARP = variable natural convection based on temperature difference (ASHRAE, Walton)
    676        \note CeilingDiffuser = ACH-based forced and mixed convection correlations
    677        \note  for ceiling diffuser configuration with simple natural convection limit
    678        \note AdaptiveConvectionAlgorithm = dynamic selection of convection models based on conditions
    679 
    680 SurfaceConvectionAlgorithm:Outside,
    681        \memo Default outside surface heat transfer convection algorithm to be used for all zones
    682        \unique-object
    683        \format singleLine
    684   A1 ; \field Algorithm
    685        \type choice
    686        \key SimpleCombined
    687        \key TARP
    688        \key MoWiTT
    689        \key DOE-2
    690        \key AdaptiveConvectionAlgorithm
    691        \default DOE-2
    692        \note SimpleCombined = Combined radiation and convection coefficient using simple ASHRAE model
    693        \note TARP = correlation from models developed by ASHRAE, Walton, and Sparrow et. al.
    694        \note MoWiTT = correlation from measurements by Klems and Yazdanian for smooth surfaces
    695        \note DOE-2 = correlation from measurements by Klems and Yazdanian for rough surfaces
    696        \note AdaptiveConvectionAlgorithm = dynamic selection of correlations based on conditions
    697 
    698 HeatBalanceAlgorithm,
    699        \memo Determines which Heat Balance Algorithm will be used ie.
    700        \memo CTF (Conduction Transfer Functions),
    701        \memo EMPD (Effective Moisture Penetration Depth with Conduction Transfer Functions).
    702        \memo Advanced/Research Usage: CondFD (Conduction Finite Difference)
    703        \memo Advanced/Research Usage: ConductionFiniteDifferenceSimplified
    704        \memo Advanced/Research Usage: HAMT (Combined Heat And Moisture Finite Element)
    705        \unique-object
    706        \format singleLine
    707   A1 , \field Algorithm
    708        \type choice
    709        \key ConductionTransferFunction
    710        \key MoisturePenetrationDepthConductionTransferFunction
    711        \key ConductionFiniteDifference
    712        \key CombinedHeatAndMoistureFiniteElement
    713        \default ConductionTransferFunction
    714   N1 , \field Surface Temperature Upper Limit
    715        \type real
    716        \minimum 200
    717        \default 200
    718        \units C
    719   N2 , \field Minimum Surface Convection Heat Transfer Coefficient Value
    720        \units W/m2-K
    721        \default 0.1
    722        \minimum> 0.0
    723   N3 ; \field Maximum Surface Convection Heat Transfer Coefficient Value
    724        \units W/m2-K
    725        \default 1000
    726        \minimum 1.0
    727 
    728 HeatBalanceSettings:ConductionFiniteDifference,
    729        \memo Determines settings for the Conduction Finite Difference
    730        \memo algorithm for surface heat transfer modeling.
    731        \unique-object
    732   A1 , \field Difference Scheme
    733        \type choice
    734        \key CrankNicholsonSecondOrder
    735        \key FullyImplicitFirstOrder
    736        \default FullyImplicitFirstOrder
    737   N1 , \field Space Discretization Constant
    738        \note increase or decrease number of nodes
    739        \type real
    740        \default 3
    741   N2 , \field Relaxation Factor
    742        \type real
    743        \default 1.0
    744        \minimum 0.01
    745        \maximum 1.0
    746   N3 ; \field Inside Face Surface Temperature Convergence Criteria
    747        \type real
    748        \default 0.002
    749        \minimum 1.0E-7
    750        \maximum 0.01
    751 
    752 ZoneAirHeatBalanceAlgorithm,
    753        \memo Determines which algorithm will be used to solve the zone air heat balance.
    754        \unique-object
    755        \format singleLine
    756        \min-fields 1
    757   A1 ; \field Algorithm
    758        \type choice
    759        \key ThirdOrderBackwardDifference
    760        \key AnalyticalSolution
    761        \key EulerMethod
    762        \default ThirdOrderBackwardDifference
    763 
    764 ZoneAirContaminantBalance,
    765        \memo Determines which contaminant concentration will be simulates.
    766        \unique-object
    767        \format singleLine
    768   A1 , \field Carbon Dioxide Concentration
    769        \note If Yes, CO2 simulation will be performed.
    770        \type choice
    771        \key Yes
    772        \key No
    773        \default No
    774   A2 , \field Outdoor Carbon Dioxide Schedule Name
    775        \note Schedule values should be in parts per million (ppm)
    776        \type object-list
    777        \object-list ScheduleNames
    778   A3 , \field Generic Contaminant Concentration
    779        \note If Yes, generic contaminant simulation will be performed.
    780        \type choice
    781        \key Yes
    782        \key No
    783        \default No
    784   A4 ; \field Outdoor Generic Contaminant Schedule Name
    785        \note Schedule values should be generic contaminant concentration in parts per
    786        \note million (ppm)
    787        \type object-list
    788        \object-list ScheduleNames
    789 
    790 ZoneAirMassFlowConservation,
    791        \memo Enforces the zone air mass flow balance by adjusting zone mixing object and/or
    792        \memo infiltration object mass flow rates.
    793        \memo If either mixing or infiltration is active, then the zone air mass flow
    794        \memo balance calculation will attempt to enforce conservation of mass for each zone.
    795        \memo If mixing is "No" and infiltration is "None", then the zone air mass flow
    796        \memo calculation defaults to assume self-balanced simple flow mixing and infiltration objects.
    797        \unique-object
    798        \min-fields 3
    799   A1,  \field Adjust Zone Mixing For Zone Air Mass Flow Balance
    800        \note If Yes, Zone mixing object flow rates are adjusted to balance the zone air mass flow
    801        \note and additional infiltration air flow may be added if required in order to balance the
    802        \note zone air mass flow.
    803        \type choice
    804        \key Yes
    805        \key No
    806        \default No
    807   A2,  \field Infiltration Balancing Method
    808        \note This input field allows user to choose how zone infiltration flow is treated during
    809        \note the zone air mass flow balance calculation.
    810        \type choice
    811        \key AddInfiltrationFlow
    812        \key AdjustInfiltrationFlow
    813        \key None
    814        \default AddInfiltrationFlow
    815        \note AddInfiltrationFlow may add infiltration to the base flow specified in the
    816        \note infiltration object to balance the zone air mass flow. The additional infiltration
    817        \note air mass flow is not self-balanced. The base flow is assumed to be self-balanced.
    818        \note AdjustInfiltrationFlow may adjust the base flow calculated using
    819        \note the base flow specified in the infiltration object to balance the zone air mass flow. If it
    820        \note If no adjustment is required, then the base infiltration is assumed to be self-balanced.
    821        \note None will make no changes to the base infiltration flow.
    822   A3;  \field Infiltration Balancing Zones
    823        \note This input field allows user to choose which zones are included in infiltration balancing.
    824        \note MixingSourceZonesOnly allows infiltration balancing only in zones which as source zones for mixing
    825        \note which also have an infiltration object defined.
    826        \note AllZones allows infiltration balancing in any zone which has an infiltration object defined.
    827        \type choice
    828        \key MixingSourceZonesOnly
    829        \key AllZones
    830        \default MixingSourceZonesOnly
    831 
    832 ZoneCapacitanceMultiplier:ResearchSpecial,
    833        \format singleLine
    834        \memo Multiplier altering the relative capacitance of the air compared to an empty zone
    835        \min-fields 6
    836   A1 , \field Name
    837        \required-field
    838        \type alpha
    839   A2 , \field Zone or ZoneList Name
    840        \type object-list
    841        \object-list ZoneAndZoneListNames
    842        \note If this field is left blank, the multipliers are applied to all the zones not specified
    843   N1 , \field Temperature Capacity Multiplier
    844        \type real
    845        \default 1.0
    846        \minimum> 0.0
    847        \note Used to alter the capacitance of zone air with respect to heat or temperature
    848   N2 , \field Humidity Capacity Multiplier
    849        \type real
    850        \default 1.0
    851        \minimum> 0.0
    852        \note Used to alter the capacitance of zone air with respect to moisture or humidity ratio
    853   N3 , \field Carbon Dioxide Capacity Multiplier
    854        \type real
    855        \default 1.0
    856        \minimum> 0.0
    857        \note Used to alter the capacitance of zone air with respect to zone air carbon dioxide concentration
    858   N4 ; \field Generic Contaminant Capacity Multiplier
    859        \type real
    860        \default 1.0
    861        \minimum> 0.0
    862        \note Used to alter the capacitance of zone air with respect to zone air generic contaminant concentration
    863 
    864 Timestep,
    865        \memo Specifies the "basic" timestep for the simulation. The
    866        \memo value entered here is also known as the Zone Timestep.  This is used in
    867        \memo the Zone Heat Balance Model calculation as the driving timestep for heat
    868        \memo transfer and load calculations.
    869        \unique-object
    870        \format singleLine
    871   N1 ; \field Number of Timesteps per Hour
    872        \note Number in hour: normal validity 4 to 60: 6 suggested
    873        \note Must be evenly divisible into 60
    874        \note Allowable values include 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30, and 60
    875        \note Normal 6 is minimum as lower values may cause inaccuracies
    876        \note A minimum value of 20 is suggested for both ConductionFiniteDifference
    877        \note and CombinedHeatAndMoistureFiniteElement surface heat balance algorithms
    878        \note A minimum of 12 is suggested for simulations involving a Vegetated Roof (Material:RoofVegetation).
    879        \default 6
    880        \type integer
    881        \minimum 1
    882        \maximum 60
    883 
    884 ConvergenceLimits,
    885        \memo Specifies limits on HVAC system simulation timesteps and iterations.
    886        \memo This item is an advanced feature that should be used only with caution.
    887        \unique-object
    888   N1 , \field Minimum System Timestep
    889        \units minutes
    890        \type integer
    891        \note 0 sets the minimum to the zone timestep (ref: Timestep)
    892        \note 1 is normal (ratchet down to 1 minute)
    893        \note setting greater than zone timestep (in minutes) will effectively set to zone timestep
    894        \minimum 0
    895        \maximum 60
    896   N2 , \field Maximum HVAC Iterations
    897        \type integer
    898        \default 20
    899        \minimum 1
    900   N3 , \field Minimum Plant Iterations
    901        \note Controls the minimum number of plant system solver iterations within a single HVAC iteration
    902        \note Larger values will increase runtime but might improve solution accuracy for complicated plant systems
    903        \note Complex plants include: several interconnected loops, heat recovery, thermal load following generators, etc.
    904        \type integer
    905        \default 2
    906        \minimum 1
    907   N4 ; \field Maximum Plant Iterations
    908        \note Controls the maximum number of plant system solver iterations within a single HVAC iteration
    909        \note Smaller values might decrease runtime but could decrease solution accuracy for complicated plant systems
    910        \type integer
    911        \default 8
    912        \minimum 2
    913 
    914 HVACSystemRootFindingAlgorithm,
    915        \memo Specifies a HVAC system solver algorithm to find a root
    916        \unique-object
    917   A1 , \field Algorithm
    918        \type choice
    919        \key RegulaFalsi
    920        \key Bisection
    921        \key BisectionThenRegulaFalsi
    922        \key RegulaFalsiThenBisection
    923        \key Alternation
    924        \default RegulaFalsi
    925   N1 ; \field Number of Iterations Before Algorithm Switch
    926        \note This field is used when RegulaFalsiThenBisection or BisectionThenRegulaFalsi is
    927        \note entered. When iteration number is greater than the value, algorithm switches.
    928        \type integer
    929        \default 5
    930 
    931 \group Compliance Objects
    932 
    933 Compliance:Building,
    934        \memo Building level inputs related to compliance to building standards, building codes, and beyond energy code programs.
    935        \unique-object
    936        \min-fields 1
    937   N1;  \field Building Rotation for Appendix G
    938        \note Additional degrees of rotation to be used with the requirement in ASHRAE Standard 90.1 Appendix G
    939        \note that states that the baseline building should be rotated in four directions.
    940        \units deg
    941        \type real
    942        \default 0.0
    943 
    944 \group Location and Climate
    945 
    946 Site:Location,
    947        \memo Specifies the building's location. Only one location is allowed.
    948        \memo Weather data file location, if it exists, will override this object.
    949        \unique-object
    950        \min-fields 5
    951   A1 , \field Name
    952        \required-field
    953        \type alpha
    954   N1 , \field Latitude
    955        \units deg
    956        \minimum -90.0
    957        \maximum +90.0
    958        \default 0.0
    959        \note + is North, - is South, degree minutes represented in decimal (i.e. 30 minutes is .5)
    960        \type real
    961   N2 , \field Longitude
    962        \units deg
    963        \minimum -180.0
    964        \maximum +180.0
    965        \default 0.0
    966        \note - is West, + is East, degree minutes represented in decimal (i.e. 30 minutes is .5)
    967        \type real
    968   N3 , \field Time Zone
    969        \note basic these limits on the WorldTimeZone Map (2003)
    970        \units hr
    971        \minimum -12.0
    972        \maximum +14.0
    973        \default 0.0
    974        \note  Time relative to GMT. Decimal hours.
    975        \type real
    976   N4 ; \field Elevation
    977        \units m
    978        \minimum -300.0
    979        \maximum< 8900.0
    980        \default 0.0
    981        \type real
    982 
    983 Site:VariableLocation,
    984        \memo Captures the scheduling of a moving/reorienting building, or more likely a vessel
    985        \unique-object
    986        \min-fields 1
    987   A1 , \field Name
    988        \required-field
    989        \type alpha
    990   A2 , \field Building Location Latitude Schedule
    991        \note The name of a schedule that defines the latitude of the building at any time.
    992        \note If not entered, the latitude defined in the Site:Location, or the default
    993        \note latitude, will be used for the entirety of the simulation
    994        \type object-list
    995        \object-list ScheduleNames
    996   A3 , \field Building Location Longitude Schedule
    997        \note The name of a schedule that defines the longitude of the building at any time.
    998        \note If not entered, the longitude defined in the Site:Location, or the default
    999        \note longitude, will be used for the entirety of the simulation
   1000        \type object-list
   1001        \object-list ScheduleNames
   1002   A4 ; \field Building Location Orientation Schedule
   1003        \note The name of a schedule that defines the orientation of the building at any time.
   1004        \note This orientation is based on a change from the original orientation. -- NEED TO REFINE THIS
   1005        \note If not entered, the original orientation will be used for the entirety of the simulation
   1006        \type object-list
   1007        \object-list ScheduleNames
   1008 
   1009 SizingPeriod:DesignDay,
   1010       \memo The design day object creates the parameters for the program to create
   1011       \memo the 24 hour weather profile that can be used for sizing as well as
   1012       \memo running to test the other simulation parameters. Parameters in this
   1013       \memo include a date (month and day), a day type (which uses the appropriate
   1014       \memo schedules for either sizing or simple tests), min/max temperatures,
   1015       \memo wind speeds, and solar radiation values.
   1016   A1, \field Name
   1017        \type alpha
   1018        \required-field
   1019        \reference RunPeriodsAndDesignDays
   1020   N1,  \field Month
   1021        \required-field
   1022        \minimum 1
   1023        \maximum 12
   1024        \type integer
   1025   N2,  \field Day of Month
   1026        \required-field
   1027        \minimum 1
   1028        \maximum 31
   1029        \type integer
   1030        \note must be valid for Month field
   1031   A2,  \field Day Type
   1032        \required-field
   1033        \note Day Type selects the schedules appropriate for this design day
   1034        \type choice
   1035        \key Sunday
   1036        \key Monday
   1037        \key Tuesday
   1038        \key Wednesday
   1039        \key Thursday
   1040        \key Friday
   1041        \key Saturday
   1042        \key Holiday
   1043        \key SummerDesignDay
   1044        \key WinterDesignDay
   1045        \key CustomDay1
   1046        \key CustomDay2
   1047   N3,  \field Maximum Dry-Bulb Temperature
   1048        \note This field is required when field "Dry-Bulb Temperature Range Modifier Type"
   1049        \note is not "TemperatureProfileSchedule".
   1050        \units C
   1051        \minimum -90
   1052        \maximum 70
   1053        \type real
   1054   N4,  \field Daily Dry-Bulb Temperature Range
   1055        \note Must still produce appropriate maximum dry-bulb (within range)
   1056        \note This field is not needed if Dry-Bulb Temperature Range Modifier Type
   1057        \note is "delta".
   1058        \units deltaC
   1059        \minimum 0
   1060        \default 0
   1061        \type real
   1062   A3,  \field Dry-Bulb Temperature Range Modifier Type
   1063        \note Type of modifier to the dry-bulb temperature calculated for the timestep
   1064        \type choice
   1065        \key MultiplierSchedule
   1066        \key DifferenceSchedule
   1067        \key TemperatureProfileSchedule
   1068        \key DefaultMultipliers
   1069        \default DefaultMultipliers
   1070   A4,  \field Dry-Bulb Temperature Range Modifier Day Schedule Name
   1071        \type object-list
   1072        \object-list DayScheduleNames
   1073        \note Only used when previous field is "MultiplierSchedule", "DifferenceSchedule" or
   1074        \note   "TemperatureProfileSchedule".
   1075        \note For type "MultiplierSchedule"  the hour/time interval values should specify
   1076        \note   the fraction (0-1) of the dry-bulb temperature range to be subtracted
   1077        \note   from the maximum dry-bulb temperature for each timestep in the day
   1078        \note For type "DifferenceSchedule" the values should specify a number to be subtracted
   1079        \note   from the maximum dry-bulb temperature for each timestep in the day.
   1080        \note Note that numbers in the difference schedule cannot be negative as that
   1081        \note   would result in a higher maximum than the maximum previously specified.
   1082        \note For type "TemperatureProfileSchedule" the values should specify the actual dry-bulb
   1083        \note   temperature for each timestep in the day.
   1084   A5,  \field Humidity Condition Type
   1085        \note values/schedules indicated here and in subsequent fields create the humidity
   1086        \note values in the 24 hour design day conditions profile.
   1087        \type choice
   1088        \key WetBulb
   1089        \key DewPoint
   1090        \key HumidityRatio
   1091        \key Enthalpy
   1092        \key RelativeHumiditySchedule
   1093        \key WetBulbProfileMultiplierSchedule
   1094        \key WetBulbProfileDifferenceSchedule
   1095        \key WetBulbProfileDefaultMultipliers
   1096        \default WetBulb
   1097   N5,  \field Wetbulb or DewPoint at Maximum Dry-Bulb
   1098        \note Wetbulb or dewpoint temperature coincident with the maximum temperature.
   1099        \note Required only if field Humidity Condition Type is "Wetbulb", "Dewpoint",
   1100        \note "WetBulbProfileMultiplierSchedule", "WetBulbProfileDifferenceSchedule",
   1101        \note or "WetBulbProfileDefaultMultipliers"
   1102        \type real
   1103        \units C
   1104   A6,  \field Humidity Condition Day Schedule Name
   1105        \type object-list
   1106        \object-list DayScheduleNames
   1107        \note Only used when Humidity Condition Type is "RelativeHumiditySchedule",
   1108        \note  "WetBulbProfileMultiplierSchedule", or "WetBulbProfileDifferenceSchedule"
   1109        \note For type "RelativeHumiditySchedule", the hour/time interval values should specify
   1110        \note   relative humidity (percent) from 0.0 to 100.0.
   1111        \note For type "WetBulbProfileMultiplierSchedule" the hour/time interval values should specify
   1112        \note   the fraction (0-1) of the wet-bulb temperature range to be subtracted from the
   1113        \note   maximum wet-bulb temperature for each timestep in the day (units = Fraction)
   1114        \note For type "WetBulbProfileDifferenceSchedule" the values should specify a number to be subtracted
   1115        \note   from the maximum wet-bulb temperature for each timestep in the day. (units = deltaC)
   1116   N6,  \field Humidity Ratio at Maximum Dry-Bulb
   1117        \note Humidity ratio coincident with the maximum temperature (constant humidity ratio throughout day).
   1118        \note Required only if field Humidity Condition Type is "HumidityRatio".
   1119        \type real
   1120        \units kgWater/kgDryAir
   1121   N7,  \field Enthalpy at Maximum Dry-Bulb
   1122        \note Enthalpy coincident with the maximum temperature.
   1123        \note Required only if field Humidity Condition Type is "Enthalpy".
   1124        \type real
   1125        \units J/kg
   1126   N8,  \field Daily Wet-Bulb Temperature Range
   1127        \units deltaC
   1128        \note Required only if Humidity Condition Type = "WetbulbProfileMultiplierSchedule" or
   1129        \note "WetBulbProfileDefaultMultipliers"
   1130   N9,  \field Barometric Pressure
   1131        \note This field's value is also checked against the calculated "standard barometric pressure"
   1132        \note for the location.  If out of range (>10%) or blank, then is replaced by standard value.
   1133        \units Pa
   1134        \minimum 31000
   1135        \maximum 120000
   1136        \type real
   1137        \ip-units inHg
   1138   N10, \field Wind Speed
   1139        \required-field
   1140        \units m/s
   1141        \minimum 0
   1142        \maximum 40
   1143        \ip-units miles/hr
   1144        \type real
   1145   N11, \field Wind Direction
   1146        \required-field
   1147        \units deg
   1148        \minimum 0
   1149        \maximum 360
   1150        \note North=0.0 East=90.0
   1151        \note 0 and 360 are the same direction.
   1152        \type real
   1153   A7,  \field Rain Indicator
   1154        \note Yes is raining (all day), No is not raining
   1155        \type choice
   1156        \key Yes
   1157        \key No
   1158        \default No
   1159   A8,  \field Snow Indicator
   1160        \type choice
   1161        \key Yes
   1162        \key No
   1163        \default No
   1164        \note Yes is Snow on Ground, No is no Snow on Ground
   1165   A9,  \field Daylight Saving Time Indicator
   1166        \note Yes -- use schedules modified for Daylight Saving Time Schedules.
   1167        \note No - do not use schedules modified for Daylight Saving Time Schedules
   1168        \type choice
   1169        \key Yes
   1170        \key No
   1171        \default No
   1172   A10, \field Solar Model Indicator
   1173        \type choice
   1174        \key ASHRAEClearSky
   1175        \key ZhangHuang
   1176        \key Schedule
   1177        \key ASHRAETau
   1178        \key ASHRAETau2017
   1179        \default ASHRAEClearSky
   1180   A11, \field Beam Solar Day Schedule Name
   1181        \note if Solar Model Indicator = Schedule, then beam schedule name (for day)
   1182        \type object-list
   1183        \object-list DayScheduleNames
   1184   A12, \field Diffuse Solar Day Schedule Name
   1185        \note if Solar Model Indicator = Schedule, then diffuse schedule name (for day)
   1186        \type object-list
   1187        \object-list DayScheduleNames
   1188   N12, \field ASHRAE Clear Sky Optical Depth for Beam Irradiance (taub)
   1189        \units dimensionless
   1190        \note Required if Solar Model Indicator = ASHRAETau or ASHRAETau2017
   1191        \note ASHRAETau2017 solar model can be used with 2013 and 2017 HOF matching taub
   1192        \minimum 0
   1193        \maximum 1.2
   1194        \default 0
   1195   N13, \field ASHRAE Clear Sky Optical Depth for Diffuse Irradiance (taud)
   1196        \units dimensionless
   1197        \note Required if Solar Model Indicator = ASHRAETau or ASHRAETau2017
   1198        \note ASHRAETau2017 solar model can be used with 2013 and 2017 HOF matching taud
   1199        \minimum 0
   1200        \maximum 3
   1201        \default 0
   1202   N14, \field Sky Clearness
   1203        \note Used if Sky Model Indicator = ASHRAEClearSky or ZhangHuang
   1204        \minimum 0.0
   1205        \maximum 1.2
   1206        \default 0.0
   1207        \note 0.0 is totally unclear, 1.0 is totally clear
   1208        \type real
   1209   N15, \field Maximum Number Warmup Days
   1210        \note If used this design day will be run with a custom limit on the maximum number of days that are repeated for warmup.
   1211        \note Limiting the number of warmup days can improve run time.
   1212        \type integer
   1213   A13; \field Begin Environment Reset Mode
   1214        \note If used this can control if you want the thermal history to be reset at the beginning of the design day.
   1215        \note When using a series of similiar design days, this field can be used to retain warmup state from the previous design day.
   1216        \type choice
   1217        \key FullResetAtBeginEnvironment
   1218        \key SuppressAllBeginEnvironmentResets
   1219        \default FullResetAtBeginEnvironment
   1220 
   1221 SizingPeriod:WeatherFileDays,
   1222        \memo Use a weather file period for design sizing calculations.
   1223   A1 , \field Name
