  18763 \group Detailed Ground Heat Transfer
  18764 
  18765 GroundHeatTransfer:Control,
  18766   \memo Object determines if the Slab and Basement preprocessors
  18767   \memo are going to be executed.
  18768   A1 , \field Name
  18769     \note This field is included for consistency.11
  18770   A2 , \field Run Basement Preprocessor
  18771        \type choice
  18772        \key Yes
  18773        \key No
  18774        \default No
  18775   A3 ; \field Run Slab Preprocessor
  18776        \type choice
  18777        \key Yes
  18778        \key No
  18779        \default No
  18780 
  18781 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:Materials,
  18782     \memo Object gives an overall description of the slab ground heat transfer model.
  18783         N1, \field NMAT: Number of materials
  18784             \note This field specifies the number of different materials that will be used in the model.
  18785             \note Typically only a ground material and a slab material are used. (2 materials)
  18786             \minimum> 0
  18787           \required-field
  18788         N2, \field ALBEDO: Surface Albedo: No Snow
  18789             \note Two fields specify the albedo value of the surface: first for no snow coverage days;
  18790             \note second for days with snow coverage. The albedo is the solar reflectivity of the surface,
  18791             \note and can vary from 0.05 for blacktop to 0.95 for fresh snow.
  18792             \note Typical values for North America reported by Bahnfleth range from 0.16 to 0.4.
  18793             \minimum 0.0
  18794             \maximum 1.0
  18795           \default 0.16
  18796         N3, \field ALBEDO: Surface Albedo: Snow
  18797             \minimum 0.0
  18798             \maximum 1.0
  18799           \default 0.40
  18800         N4, \field EPSLW: Surface Emissivity: No Snow
  18801           \note EPSLW (No Snow and Snow) specifies the long wavelength (thermal) emissivity of the ground surface.
  18802           \note primarily important for nighttime radiation to sky.
  18803           \note typical value .95
  18804             \minimum> 0.0
  18805           \default 0.94
  18806         N5, \field EPSLW: Surface Emissivity: Snow
  18807             \minimum> 0.0
  18808           \default 0.86
  18809         N6, \field Z0: Surface Roughness: No Snow
  18810           \note fields Z0 (No Snow and Snow) describe the height at which an experimentally velocity profile goes to zero.
  18811           \note typical value= .75 cm
  18812          \minimum> 0.0
  18813           \default .75
  18814           \units cm
  18815         N7, \field Z0: Surface Roughness: Snow
  18816             \note typical value= .05 cm
  18817          \minimum> 0.0
  18818           \default 0.25
  18819           \units cm
  18820         N8, \field HIN: Indoor HConv: Downward Flow
  18821             \note These fields specify the combined convective and radiative heat transfer coefficient between
  18822             \note the slab top inside surface and the room air for the cases where heat is flowing downward,
  18823             \note and upward. The program toggles between the two if the direction of the heat flux changes.
  18824             \note Typical values can be found in the ASHRAE Handbook of Fundamentals, but should be
  18825             \note about 6 W/(m2-K) for downward heat flow and 9 W/(m2-K) for upward heat flow.
  18826             \note typical value= 4-10
  18827             \units W/m2-K
  18828             \minimum> 0.0
  18829           \default 6.13
  18830         N9; \field HIN: Indoor HConv: Upward
  18831             \minimum> 0.0
  18832             \note typical value= 4-10
  18833             \units W/m2-K
  18834           \default 9.26
  18835 
  18836 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:MatlProps,
  18837     \memo This object contains the material properties for the materials
  18838     \memo used in the model. The fields are mostly self explanatory.
  18839      N1,  \field RHO: Slab Material density
  18840           \note Density of Slab Material
  18841           \note typical value= 2300.0
  18842           \units kg/m3
  18843           \minimum> 0.0
  18844           \default 2300
  18845      N2,  \field RHO: Soil Density
  18846           \note Density of Soil Material
  18847           \note typical value= 1200.0
  18848           \minimum> 0.0
  18849           \default 1200
  18850           \units kg/m3
  18851      N3,  \field CP: Slab CP
  18852           \note Specific Heat of Slab Material
  18853           \note typical value=650.0
  18854           \units J/kg-K
  18855           \minimum> 0.0
  18856           \default 650
  18857      N4,  \field CP: Soil CP
  18858           \note Specific Heat of Soil Material
  18859           \note typical value= 1200.0
  18860           \minimum> 0.0
  18861           \default 1200
  18862           \units J/kg-K
  18863      N5,  \field TCON: Slab k
  18864           \note Conductivity of Slab Material
  18865           \note typical value= .9
  18866           \minimum> 0.0
  18867           \units W/m-K
  18868           \default 0.9
  18869      N6;  \field TCON: Soil k
  18870           \note Conductivity of Soil Material
  18871           \note typical value= 1.0
  18872           \minimum> 0.0
  18873           \units W/m-K
  18874           \default 1.0
  18875 
  18876 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:BoundConds,
  18877       \memo Supplies some of the boundary conditions used in the ground heat transfer calculations.
  18878         A1, \field EVTR: Is surface evapotranspiration modeled
  18879             \note This field specifies whether or not to use the evapotransporation model.
  18880             \note The inclusion of evapotransporation in the calculation has the greatest
  18881             \note effect in warm dry climates, primarily on the ground surface temperature.
  18882             \note This field can be used to turn the evapotransporation off and on to check
  18883             \note sensitivity to it.
  18884             \type choice
  18885             \key TRUE
  18886             \key FALSE
  18887           \required-field
  18888         A2, \field FIXBC: is the lower boundary at a fixed temperature
  18889             \note This field permits using a fixed temperature at the lower surface of the model
  18890             \note instead of a zero heat flux condition. This change normally has a very small
  18891             \note effect on the results.
  18892             \note FALSE selects the zero flux lower boundary condition
  18893             \type choice
  18894             \key TRUE
  18895             \key FALSE
  18896           \required-field
  18897         N1, \field TDEEPin
  18898             \note User input lower boundary temperature if FIXBC is TRUE
  18899             \note Blank for FIXBC FALSE or to use the calculated 1-D deep ground temperature.
  18900             \units C
  18901         A3, \field USRHflag: Is the ground surface h specified by the user?
  18902             \note This field flags the use of a user specified heat transfer coefficient
  18903             \note on the ground surface. This condition is used primarily for testing.
  18904             \note For normal runs (USPHflag is FALSE) and the program calculates the heat
  18905             \note transfer coefficient using the weather conditions.
  18906             \type choice
  18907             \key TRUE
  18908             \key FALSE
  18909           \required-field
  18910         N2; \field USERH: User specified ground surface heat transfer coefficient
  18911             \note Used only if USRHflag is TRUE and the heat transfer coefficient value is
  18912             \note specified in this field.
  18913             \units W/m2-K
  18914 
  18915 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:BldgProps,
  18916           \memo Object provides information about the building and its operating conditions
  18917           \memo Monthly Average Temperature SetPoint fields
  18918           \memo specify the average indoor building set point temperatures for each
  18919           \memo month of the year. These fields are useful for simulating a building
  18920           \memo that is not temperature controlled for some of the year.
  18921           \memo In such a case, the average indoor set point temperatures
  18922           \memo can be obtained by first running the model in EnergyPlus with an
  18923           \memo insulated floor boundary condition, and then using the resulting
  18924           \memo monthly average zone temperatures in these fields.
  18925       N1, \field IYRS: Number of years to iterate
  18926           \note This field specifies the number of years to iterate.
  18927           \note Either the ground heat transfer calculations come to an
  18928           \note an annual steady periodic condition by converging to a tolerance
  18929           \note (see ConvTol field) or it runs for this number of years.
  18930           \note A ten year maximum is usually sufficient.
  18931           \default 10
  18932           \minimum 1
  18933       N2, \field Shape: Slab shape
  18934           \note Use only the value 0 here. Only a rectangular shape is implemented.
  18935           \minimum 0
  18936           \maximum 0
  18937       N3, \field HBLDG: Building height
  18938           \note This field supplies the building height. This is used to calculate
  18939           \note the building shadowing on the ground.
  18940           \minimum 0
  18941           \note typical value= 0-20
  18942           \units m
  18943       N4, \field TIN1: January Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18944           \note see memo on object for more information
  18945           \default 22
  18946           \units C
  18947       N5, \field TIN2: February Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18948           \note see memo on object for more information
  18949           \default 22
  18950           \units C
  18951       N6, \field TIN3: March Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18952           \note see memo on object for more information
  18953           \default 22
  18954           \units C
  18955       N7, \field TIN4: April Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18956           \note see memo on object for more information
  18957           \default 22
  18958           \units C
  18959       N8, \field TIN5: May Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18960           \note see memo on object for more information
  18961           \default 22
  18962           \units C
  18963       N9, \field TIN6: June Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18964           \note see memo on object for more information
  18965           \default 22
  18966           \units C
  18967       N10,\field TIN7: July Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18968           \note see memo on object for more information
  18969           \default 22
  18970           \units C
  18971       N11,\field TIN8: August Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18972           \note see memo on object for more information
  18973           \default 22
  18974           \units C
  18975       N12,\field TIN9: September Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18976           \note see memo on object for more information
  18977           \default 22
  18978           \units C
  18979       N13,\field TIN10: October Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18980           \note see memo on object for more information
  18981           \default 22
  18982           \units C
  18983       N14,\field TIN11: November Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18984           \note see memo on object for more information
  18985           \default 22
  18986           \units C
  18987       N15,\field TIN12: December Indoor Average Temperature Setpoint
  18988           \note see memo on object for more information
  18989           \default 22
  18990           \units C
  18991       N16,\field TINAmp: Daily Indoor sine wave variation amplitude
  18992           \note This field permits imposing a daily sinusoidal variation
  18993           \note in the indoor setpoint temperature to simulate the effect
  18994           \note of a setback profile.
  18995           \note The value specified is the amplitude of the sine wave.
  18996           \default 0
  18997           \units deltaC
  18998       N17;\field ConvTol: Convergence Tolerance
  18999           \note This field specifies the convergence tolerance used to
  19000           \note control the iteration. When the temperature change of all nodes
  19001           \note is less than the convergence value, iteration ceases.
  19002           \default 0.1
  19003 
  19004 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:Insulation,
  19005     \memo This object supplies the information about insulation used around the slab.
  19006     \memo There are two possible configurations: under the slab or vertical insulation
  19007     \memo around the slab.
  19008     \min-fields 5
  19009         N1, \field RINS: R value of under slab insulation
  19010             \note This field provides the thermal resistance value
  19011             \note of the under slab insulation. It should be zero
  19012             \note if the vertical insulation configuration is selected.
  19013             \note typical value= 0-2.0
  19014             \units m2-K/W
  19015           \default 0.0
  19016         N2, \field DINS: Width of strip of under slab insulation
  19017             \note This specifies the width of the perimeter strip of insulation
  19018             \note under the slab. It should be zero if for the vertical insulation
  19019             \note configuration is selected.
  19020             \note typical value= 0-2.0
  19021             \units m
  19022           \default 0.0
  19023         N3, \field RVINS: R value of vertical insulation
  19024             \note This field specifies the thermal resistance of the vertical
  19025             \note insulation. It should be zero if the under slab insulation
  19026             \note configuration is selected.
  19027             \note typical value= 0-3.0
  19028             \units m2-K/W
  19029           \default 0.0
  19030         N4, \field ZVINS: Depth of vertical insulation
  19031             \note This field specifies the depth of the vertical insulation
  19032             \note into the ground in meters. It starts at the slab upper surface
  19033             \note and extends into the ground.
  19034             \note It should be zero if the under slab insulation
  19035             \note configuration is selected.
  19036             \note only use values= .2 .4 .6 .8 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0
  19037             \units m
  19038           \default 0
  19039         N5; \field IVINS: Flag: Is there vertical insulation
  19040             \note Specifies if the vertical insulation configuration is being used.
  19041             \note values: 1=yes vertical insulation 0=no under-slab insulation
  19042           \type choice
  19043           \key 0
  19044           \key 1
  19045           \default 0
  19046 
  19047 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:EquivalentSlab,
  19048     \memo Using an equivalent slab allows non-rectangular shapes to be modeled accurately.
  19049     \memo Object uses the area - perimeter (area/perimeter) ratio to determine the
  19050     \memo size of an equivalent rectangular slab.
  19051     \memo EnergyPlus users normally use this option.
  19052      N1,  \field APRatio: The area to perimeter ratio for this slab
  19053           \note Equivalent square slab is simulated,  side is 4*APRatio.
  19054           \units m
  19055           \minimum 1.5
  19056           \maximum 22
  19057           \required-field
  19058      N2,  \field SLABDEPTH: Thickness of slab on grade
  19059           \note This field specifies the thickness of the slab. The slab top surface is level
  19060           \note with the ground surface, so this is the depth into the ground.
  19061           \note The slab depth has a significant effect on the temperature calculation,
  19062           \note and it is also important for the auto-grid process.
  19063           \note The finite difference grids are set in such a way that they use the slab thickness
  19064           \note to determine the vertical grid spacing. Autogridding will fail if the slab thickness
  19065           \note is specified larger than 0.25 meters.
  19066           \default 0.1
  19067           \units m
  19068      N3,  \field CLEARANCE: Distance from edge of slab to domain edge
  19069           \note This field specifies the distance from the slab to the edge of
  19070           \note the area that will be modeled with the grid system. It is the basic size
  19071           \note dimension that is used to set the horizontal extent of the domain.
  19072           \note 15 meters is a reasonable value.
  19073           \default 15.0
  19074           \units m
  19075      N4;  \field ZCLEARANCE: Distance from bottom of slab to domain bottom
  19076           \note This field specifies the vertical distance from the slab to the
  19077           \note bottom edge of the area that will be modeled with the grid system.
  19078           \note 15 meters is a reasonable value.
  19079           \default 15.0
  19080           \units m
  19081 
  19082 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:AutoGrid,
  19083             \memo AutoGrid only necessary when EquivalentSlab option not chosen.
  19084             \memo  Not normally needed by EnergyPlus users.
  19085             \memo  This object permits user selection of rectangular slab dimensions.
  19086             \memo  NO SLAB DIMENSIONS LESS THAN 6 m.
  19087         N1, \field SLABX: X dimension of the building slab
  19088             \minimum 6
  19089             \note typical values= 6 to 60.0
  19090             \units m
  19091           \required-field
  19092         N2, \field SLABY: Y dimension of the building slab
  19093             \minimum 6
  19094             \note typical values= 6 to 60.0
  19095             \units m
  19096           \required-field
  19097         N3, \field SLABDEPTH: Thickness of slab on grade
  19098             \default 0.1
  19099             \units m
  19100         N4, \field CLEARANCE: Distance from edge of slab to domain edge
  19101             \default 15.0
  19102             \units m
  19103         N5; \field ZCLEARANCE: Distance from bottom of slab to domain bottom
  19104             \default 15.0
  19105             \units m
  19106 
  19107 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:ManualGrid,
  19108             \memo Manual Grid only necessary when using manual gridding (not recommended)
  19109             \memo Used only in special cases when previous two objects are not used.
  19110             \memo User must input complete gridding information.
  19111         N1, \field NX: Number of cells in the X direction
  19112           \minimum 1
  19113           \required-field
  19114         N2, \field NY: Number of cells in the Y direction
  19115           \minimum 1
  19116           \required-field
  19117         N3, \field NZ: Number of cells in the Z direction
  19118           \minimum 1
  19119           \required-field
  19120         N4, \field IBOX: X direction cell indicator of slab edge
  19121             \note typical values= 1-10
  19122           \required-field
  19123         N5; \field JBOX: Y direction cell indicator of slab edge
  19124             \note typical values= 1-10
  19125           \required-field
  19126 
  19127 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:XFACE,
  19128          \memo This is only needed when using manual gridding (not recommended)
  19129          \memo XFACE: X Direction cell face coordinates: m
  19130         N1, N2, N3, N4, N5, N6, N7, N8, N9, N10, N11, N12, N13, N14,                  \note fields as indicated
  19131         N15, N16, N17, N18, N19, N20, N21, N22, N23, N24, N25, N26, N27, N28, N29,    \note fields as indicated
  19132         N30, N31, N32, N33, N34, N35, N36, N37, N38, N39, N40;                        \note fields as indicated
  19133 
  19134 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:YFACE,
  19135          \memo This is only needed when using manual gridding (not recommended)
  19136          \memo YFACE: Y Direction cell face coordinates: m,
  19137         N1, N2, N3, N4, N5, N6, N7, N8, N9, N10, N11, N12, N13, N14,                  \note fields as indicated
  19138         N15, N16, N17, N18, N19, N20, N21, N22, N23, N24, N25, N26, N27, N28, N29,    \note fields as indicated
  19139         N30, N31, N32, N33, N34, N35, N36, N37, N38, N39, N40;                        \note fields as indicated
  19140 
  19141 GroundHeatTransfer:Slab:ZFACE,
  19142          \memo This is only needed when using manual gridding (not recommended)
  19143          \memo ZFACE: Z Direction cell face coordinates: m
  19144         N1, N2, N3, N4, N5, N6, N7, N8, N9, N10, N11, N12, N13, N14,                  \note fields as indicated
  19145         N15, N16, N17, N18, N19, N20, N21, N22, N23, N24, N25;                        \note fields as indicated
  19146 
  19147 GroundHeatTransfer:Basement:SimParameters,
  19148           \memo Specifies certain parameters that control the Basement preprocessor ground heat
  19149           \memo transfer simulation.
  19150      N1,  \field F: Multiplier for the ADI solution
  19151          \note 0<F<1.0,
  19152           \note typically 0.1 (0.3 for high k soil - saturated sand is about 2.6 w/m-K)
  19153           \minimum> 0.0
  19154           \maximum 1.0
  19155       N2; \field IYRS: Maximum number of yearly iterations:
  19156          \note typically 15-30]
  19157           \minimum 0
  19158           \default 15
  19159 
  19160 GroundHeatTransfer:Basement:MatlProps,
  19161          \memo Specifies the material properties for the Basement preprocessor ground heat
  19162          \memo transfer simulation. Only the Foundation Wall, Floor Slab, Soil,
  19163          \memo and Gravel properties are currently used.
  19164      N1, \field NMAT: Number of materials in this domain
  19165          \maximum 6
  19166           \required-field
  19167      N2, \field Density for Foundation Wall
  19168          \minimum> 0.0
  19169          \default 2243
  19170          \units kg/m3
  19171      N3, \field density for Floor Slab
  19172          \minimum> 0.0
  19173          \default 2243
  19174          \units kg/m3
  19175      N4, \field density for Ceiling
  19176          \minimum> 0.0
  19177          \default 311
  19178          \units kg/m3
  19179      N5, \field density for Soil
  19180          \minimum> 0.0
  19181          \default 1500
  19182          \units kg/m3
  19183      N6, \field density for Gravel
  19184          \minimum> 0.0
  19185          \default 2000
  19186          \units kg/m3
  19187      N7, \field density for Wood
  19188          \minimum> 0.0
  19189          \default 449
  19190          \units kg/m3
  19191      N8,  \field Specific heat for foundation wall
  19192           \minimum> 0.0
  19193           \default 880
  19194           \units J/kg-K
  19195      N9,  \field Specific heat for floor slab
  19196           \minimum> 0.0
  19197           \default 880
  19198           \units J/kg-K
  19199      N10, \field Specific heat for ceiling
  19200           \minimum> 0.0
  19201           \default 1530
  19202           \units J/kg-K
  19203      N11, \field Specific heat for soil
