  58874   A3 , \field Wetbulb Effectiveness Flow Ratio Modifier Curve Name
  58875        \note this curve modifies the wetbulb effectiveness in the previous field (eff_wb_design)
  58876        \note by multiplying the value by the result of this curve, eff_wb = eff_wb_design * func(HXFlowRatio)
  58877        \note x = HXFlowRatio = sum of the primary and secondary flow rates divided by the sum of the design flow
  58878        \note rates. If this input field is left blank, constant cooler wetbulb effectiveness is assumed.
  58879        \note This input field is required in order to use operating range control based on the following three
  58880        \note input fields: Evaporative Operation Minimum Limit Secondary Air Drybulb Temperature,
  58881        \note Evaporative Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Wetbulb Temperature, and
  58882        \note Dry Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Drybulb Temperature
  58883        \type object-list
  58884        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  58885   N2 , \field Cooler Drybulb Design Effectiveness
  58886        \type real
  58887        \note dry operation effectiveness with respect to drybulb temperature difference
  58888        \note this is the nominal design dryblub effectiveness at design air flow rates, no evaporation water active
  58889        \minimum 0.0
  58890   A4 , \field Drybulb Effectiveness Flow Ratio Modifier Curve Name
  58891        \note this curve modifies the drybulb effectiveness in the previous field (eff_db_design)
  58892        \note by multiplying the value by the result of this curve, eff_db = eff_db_design * f(HXFlowRatio)
  58893        \note x = HXFlowRatio = sum of the primary and secondary flow rates divided by the sum of the design flow
  58894        \note rates. If this input field is left blank, constant cooler drybulb effectiveness is assumed.
  58895        \note This input field is required in order to use operating range control based on the following three
  58896        \note input fields: Evaporative Operation Minimum Limit Secondary Air Drybulb Temperature,
  58897        \note Evaporative Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Wetbulb Temperature, and
  58898        \note Dry Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Drybulb Temperature
  58899        \type object-list
  58900        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  58901   N3 , \field Recirculating Water Pump Design Power
  58902        \units W
  58903        \ip-units W
  58904        \note This is the nominal design pump power of water recirculation and spray for evaporation at design air flow
  58905        \note rates and cooler design effectiveness
  58906        \autosizable
  58907        \default autosize
  58908   N4 , \field Water Pump Power Sizing Factor
  58909        \type real
  58910        \units W/(m3/s)
  58911        \ip-units W/(gal/min)
  58912        \default 90.0
  58913        \note This field is used when the previous field is set to autosize. The pump power is scaled with Secondary Air
  58914        \note Design Air Flow Rate. This value was backed out from inputs in energy plus example files. Average Pump Power
  58915        \note sizing factor was estimated from pump power and secondary air design flow rates inputs from energyplus example
  58916        \note files is about 90.0 [W/(m3/s)] (=90.0 ~ Pump Power / Secondary Air Design Flow Rate). The factor ranges from
  58917        \note 55.0 to 150.0 [W/(m3/s)] were noted. The pump power can be sized to zero by setting this factor to zero.
  58918   A5 , \field Water Pump Power Modifier Curve Name
  58919        \note this curve modifies the pump power in the previous field by multiplying the design power by the result of this curve.
  58920        \note x = ff = flow fraction on the secondary side, secondary air flow rate during operation divided by Secondary Air
  58921        \note Design Air Flow Rate. If this input field is left blank, pump power is assumed to be proportional to part load ratio.
  58922        \type object-list
  58923        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  58924   N5 , \field Secondary Air Design Flow Rate
  58925        \type real
  58926        \units m3/s
  58927        \minimum> 0.0
  58928        \autosizable
  58929        \default autosize
  58930   N6 , \field Secondary Air Flow Scaling Factor
  58931        \type real
  58932        \units dimensionless
  58933        \default 1.0
  58934        \note This field is used when the previous field is set to autoize.  The Primary Design Air Flow Rate is scaled using this factor
  58935        \note to calculate the secondary design air flow rate.
  58936   N7 , \field Secondary Air Fan Design Power
  58937        \type real
  58938        \units W
  58939        \note This is the fan design power at Secondary Design Air Flow Rate. This is the nominal design power at full speed.
  58940        \autosizable
  58941        \default autosize
  58942   N8 , \field Secondary Air Fan Sizing Specific Power
  58943        \type real
  58944        \units W/(m3/s)
  58945        \default 250.0
  58946        \note This field is used when the previous field is set to autosize. The fan power is scaled with Secondary Air Design Flow Rate.
  58947        \note The default value is estimated from 125 Pa fan total pressure and fan total efficiency of 50.0% (250.0 = 125/0.5).
  58948   A6 , \field Secondary Air Fan Power Modifier Curve Name
  58949        \note this curve modifies the design fan power in the previous field by multiplying the value by the result
  58950        \note of this curve.  It should have a value of 1.0 at a x = 1.0.
  58951        \note x = ff = flow fraction on the secondary side, secondary air flow rate during operation divided by Secondary Air Design Air
  58952        \note Flow Rate. If this input field is left blank, the secondary fan power is assumed to be proportional to part load ratio.
  58953        \type object-list
  58954        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  58955   A7 , \field Primary Air Inlet Node Name
  58956        \required-field
  58957        \type node
  58958   A8 , \field Primary Air Outlet Node Name
  58959        \required-field
  58960        \type node
  58961   N9 , \field Primary Air Design Flow Rate
  58962        \type real
  58963        \units m3/s
  58964        \minimum> 0.0
  58965        \autosizable
  58966        \default autosize
  58967   N10, \field Dewpoint Effectiveness Factor
  58968        \type real
  58969        \minimum 0.0
  58970   A9 , \field Secondary Air Inlet Node Name
  58971        \required-field
  58972        \type node
  58973   A10, \field Secondary Air Outlet Node Name
  58974        \required-field
  58975        \type node
  58976   A11, \field Sensor Node Name
  58977        \required-field
  58978        \type node
  58979   A12, \field Relief Air Inlet Node Name
  58980        \type node
  58981   A13, \field Water Supply Storage Tank Name
  58982        \type object-list
  58983        \object-list WaterStorageTankNames
  58984   N11, \field Drift Loss Fraction
  58985        \type real
  58986        \minimum 0.0
  58987        \note Rate of drift loss as a fraction of evaporated water flow rate.
  58988        \note If this input field is left blank, then zero drift loss is assumed.
  58989        \default 0.0
  58990   N12, \field Blowdown Concentration Ratio
  58991        \type real
  58992        \minimum 2.0
  58993        \note Characterizes the rate of blowdown in the evaporative cooler.
  58994        \note Blowdown is water intentionally drained from the cooler in order to offset the build
  58995        \note up of solids in the water that would otherwise occur because of evaporation.
  58996        \note Ratio of solids in the blowdown water to solids in the make up water.
  58997        \note A typical value is 3.  If left blank then there is no blowdown.
  58998   N13, \field Evaporative Operation Minimum Limit Secondary Air Drybulb Temperature
  58999        \type real
  59000        \note This input field value defines the secondary air inlet node drybulb temperature
  59001        \note limits in degreeCelsius. When the secondary side entering air dry bulb temperature
  59002        \note drops below this limit, then the evaporative cooler operation mode changes to dry
  59003        \note heat exchanger. Users specify their own limits. If this field is left blank, then
  59004        \note there is no drybulb temperature lower limit for evaporative cooler operation. If
  59005        \note operating range control is desired then this input field and the next two input
  59006        \note fields should be specified or all the three should be left blank or left out. If
  59007        \note no minimum drybulb temperature limit is desired while there are maximum drybulb
  59008        \note and wetbulb temperature limits then specify very low minimum temperature limit
  59009        \note value (e.g. -99.0C).
  59010   N14, \field Evaporative Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Wetbulb Temperature
  59011        \type real
  59012        \note This input field value defines the secondary air inlet node wetbulb temperature
  59013        \note limits in degree Celsius. When the secondary side entering air wet bulb temperature
  59014        \note exceeds this limit, then the evaporative cooler urns off and does not attempt to do
  59015        \note any cooling. If this field is left blank, then there is no wetbulb temperature
  59016        \note upper limit for evaporative cooler wet operation mode. If this input field is left
  59017        \note blank then, the previous and the next input fields should also be left blank. If no
  59018        \note maximum wetbulb temperature limits is desired while there are minimum drybulb and
  59019        \note maximum drybulb upper temperature limits then specify very high maximum wetbulb
  59020        \note temperature limit value (e.g. 99.0 C).
  59021   N15; \field Dry Operation Maximum Limit Outdoor Drybulb Temperature
  59022        \type real
  59023        \note This input field value defines the secondary air inlet node drybulb temperature
  59024        \note limits in degree Celsius. When the secondary side entering air drybulb temperature
  59025        \note exceeds this limit, then the evaporative cooler will not run in dry operation mode
  59026        \note or may be turned off depending on its wetbulb temperature. If this field is left
  59027        \note blank, then there is no drybulb temperature maximum limit for evaporative cooler
  59028        \note operation. If this input field is left blank then, the previous and the next input
  59029        \note fields should also be left blank. If no maximum drybulb temperature limit is
  59030        \note desired while there are minimum drybulb and maximum wetbulb upper temperature
  59031        \note limits then specify very high maximum drybulb temperature limit value (e.g. 99.0 C).
  59032 
  59033 
  59034 EvaporativeCooler:Direct:ResearchSpecial,
  59035        \memo Direct evaporative cooler with user-specified effectiveness (can represent rigid pad
  59036        \memo or similar media), and recirculating water pump, and secondary air fan. This model is
  59037        \memo controlled to meet the primary air outlet temperature setpoint.
  59038        \min-fields 11
  59039   A1 , \field Name
  59040        \type alpha
  59041        \reference EvapCoolerNames
  59042        \reference-class-name validBranchEquipmentTypes
  59043        \reference validBranchEquipmentNames
  59044        \reference-class-name validOASysEquipmentTypes
  59045        \reference validOASysEquipmentNames
  59046   A2 , \field Availability Schedule Name
  59047        \note Availability schedule name for this system. Schedule value > 0 means the system is available.
  59048        \note If this field is blank, the system is always available.
  59049        \type object-list
  59050        \object-list ScheduleNames
  59051   N1 , \field Cooler Design Effectiveness
  59052        \note effectiveness with respect to wet-bulb depression
  59053        \type real
  59054        \minimum 0.0
  59055        \maximum 1.0
  59056   A3 , \field Effectiveness Flow Ratio Modifier Curve Name
  59057        \note this curve modifies the design effectiveness in the previous field
  59058        \note by multiplying the value by the result of this curve.  The effectiveness flow modifier curve
  59059        \note is a function of flow fraction. Flow fraction is the ratio of current primary air flow rate to
  59060        \note the primary air design flow rate. If this input field is left blank then, the effectiveness
  59061        \note is assumed to be constant.
  59062        \type object-list
  59063        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  59064   N2 , \field Primary Air Design Flow Rate
  59065        \type real
  59066        \units m3/s
  59067        \minimum> 0.0
  59068        \autosizable
  59069        \default autosize
  59070   N3 , \field Recirculating Water Pump Design Power
  59071        \units W
  59072        \ip-units W
  59073        \minimum 0.0
  59074        \note This is the design water pump or spray for evaporation at the primary air design air flow rates
  59075        \note and cooler design effectiveness
  59076        \autosizable
  59077        \default autosize
  59078   N4 , \field Water Pump Power Sizing Factor
  59079        \type real
  59080        \units W/(m3/s)
  59081        \ip-units W/(gal/min)
  59082        \default 90.0
  59083        \note This field is used when the previous field is set to autosize. The pump power is scaled with Primary Air
  59084        \note Design Air Flow Rate. This value was backed out from inputs in energy plus example files. Average Pump Power
  59085        \note sizing factor was estimated from pump power and primary air design flow rates inputs from energyplus example
  59086        \note files is about 90.0 [W/(m3/s)] (=90.0 ~ Pump Power / Primary Air Design Flow Rate). The factor ranges from
  59087        \note 55.0 to 150.0 [W/(m3/s)]. The pump power can be sized to zero by setting this factor to zero.
  59088   A4 , \field Water Pump Power Modifier Curve Name
  59089        \note this curve modifies the pump power in the previous field by multiplying the design power by the result of this curve.
  59090        \note x = ff = flow fraction on the primary air. The flow fraction is the primary air flow rate during current operation divided
  59091        \note by Primary Air Design Flow Rate
  59092        \type object-list
  59093        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  59094   A5 , \field Air Inlet Node Name
  59095        \required-field
  59096        \type node
  59097   A6 , \field Air Outlet Node Name
  59098        \required-field
  59099        \type node
  59100   A7 , \field Sensor Node Name
  59101        \required-field
  59102        \type node
  59103   A8 , \field Water Supply Storage Tank Name
  59104        \type object-list
  59105        \object-list WaterStorageTankNames
  59106   N5 , \field Drift Loss Fraction
  59107        \type real
  59108        \minimum 0.0
  59109        \note Rate of drift loss as a fraction of evaporated water flow rate
  59110   N6 , \field Blowdown Concentration Ratio
  59111        \type real
  59112        \minimum 2.0
  59113        \note Characterizes the rate of blowdown in the evaporative cooler.
  59114        \note Blowdown is water intentionally drained from the cooler in order to offset the build up
  59115        \note of solids in the water that would otherwise occur because of evaporation.
  59116        \note Ratio of solids in the blowdown water to solids in the make up water.
  59117        \note A typical value is 3. If left blank then there is no blowdown.
  59118   N7 , \field Evaporative Operation Minimum Drybulb Temperature
  59119        \type real
  59120        \minimum -99.0
  59121        \note This numeric field defines the evaporative cooler air inlet node drybulb temperature minimum
  59122        \note limit in degrees Celsius. The evaporative cooler will be turned off when the evaporator cooler
  59123        \note air inlet node dry-bulb temperature falls below this limit. The typical minimum value is 16degC. Users
  59124        \note are allowed to specify their own limits. If this field is left blank, then there is no drybulb lower
  59125        \note temperature limit for evaporative cooler operation.
  59126   N8 , \field Evaporative Operation Maximum Limit Wetbulb Temperature
  59127        \type real
  59128        \note when outdoor wetbulb temperature rises above this limit the cooler shuts down.
  59129        \note This numeric field defines the evaporative cooler air inlet node wet-bulb temperature maximum
  59130        \note limit in degrees Celsius. The evaporative cooler will be turned off when the evaporative cooler
  59131        \note air inlet node wet-bulb temperature exceeds this limit. The typical maximum value is 24degC. Users
  59132        \note are allowed to specify their own limits. If this field is left blank, then there is no upper
  59133        \note wetbulb temperature limit for evaporative cooler operation.
  59134   N9 ; \field Evaporative Operation Maximum Limit Drybulb Temperature
  59135        \type real
  59136        \note This numeric field defines the evaporative cooler air inlet node dry-bulb temperature maximum
  59137        \note limit in degrees Celsius. The evaporative cooler will be turned off when its air inlet node
  59138        \note drybulb temperature exceeds this limit. The typical maximum value is 26degC. Users
  59139        \note are allowed to specify their own limits. If this field is left blank, then there is no upper
  59140        \note temperature limit for evaporative cooler operation.
  59141 
  59142 \group Humidifiers and Dehumidifiers
