  61955 \group Variable Refrigerant Flow Equipment
  61956 
  61957 AirConditioner:VariableRefrigerantFlow,
  61958        \memo Variable refrigerant flow (VRF) air-to-air heat pump condensing unit (includes one
  61959        \memo or more electric compressors and outdoor fan). Serves one or more VRF zone terminal
  61960        \memo units. See ZoneHVAC:TerminalUnit:VariableRefrigerantFlow and ZoneTerminalUnitList.
  61961        \min-fields 37
  61962   A1 , \field Heat Pump Name
  61963        \required-field
  61964        \type alpha
  61965        \reference-class-name validBranchEquipmentTypes
  61966        \reference validBranchEquipmentNames
  61967        \note Enter a unique name for this variable refrigerant flow heat pump.
  61968   A2 , \field Availability Schedule Name
  61969        \note Availability schedule name for this system. Schedule value > 0 means the system is available.
  61970        \note If this field is blank, the system is always available.
  61971        \type object-list
  61972        \object-list ScheduleNames
  61973        \note Enter the name of a schedule that defines the availability of the unit.
  61974        \note Schedule values of 0 denote the unit is off. All other values denote the unit is available.
  61975        \note If this field is left blank, the unit is available the entire simulation.
  61976   N1 , \field Gross Rated Total Cooling Capacity
  61977        \type real
  61978        \units W
  61979        \minimum> 0.0
  61980        \autosizable
  61981        \note Enter the total cooling capacity in watts at rated conditions or set to autosize.
  61982        \note Total cooling capacity not accounting for the effect of supply air fan heat
  61983   N2 , \field Gross Rated Cooling COP
  61984        \type real
  61985        \units W/W
  61986        \minimum> 0.0
  61987        \default 3.3
  61988        \note Enter the coefficient of performance at rated conditions or leave blank to use default.
  61989        \note COP includes compressor and condenser fan electrical energy input
  61990        \note COP does not include supply fan heat or supply fan electric power input
  61991   N3 , \field Minimum Outdoor Temperature in Cooling Mode
  61992        \type real
  61993        \units C
  61994        \default -6.0
  61995        \note Enter the minimum outdoor temperature allowed for cooling operation.
  61996        \note Cooling is disabled below this temperature.
  61997   N4 , \field Maximum Outdoor Temperature in Cooling Mode
  61998        \type real
  61999        \units C
  62000        \default 43.0
  62001        \note Enter the maximum outdoor temperature allowed for cooling operation.
  62002        \note Cooling is disabled above this temperature.
  62003   A3 , \field Cooling Capacity Ratio Modifier Function of Low Temperature Curve Name
  62004        \type object-list
  62005        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62006        \note Enter a curve name that represents full load cooling capacity ratio as a
  62007        \note function of outdoor dry-bulb temperature and indoor wet-bulb temperature.
  62008        \note Up to two curves are allowed if the performance cannot be represented by a single curve.
  62009        \note The following two fields are used if two curves are required.
  62010   A4 , \field Cooling Capacity Ratio Boundary Curve Name
  62011        \type object-list
  62012        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62013        \note This curve object is used to allow separate low and high cooling capacity ratio
  62014        \note performance curves. This curve represents a line passing through the points where
  62015        \note performance changes. The curve calculates outdoor dry-bulb temperature given weighted
  62016        \note average indoor wet-bulb temperature. If a single performance curve is used,
  62017        \note leave this field blank.
  62018   A5 , \field Cooling Capacity Ratio Modifier Function of High Temperature Curve Name
  62019        \type object-list
  62020        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62021        \note This curve object is used to describe the high outdoor temperature
  62022        \note performance curve used to describe cooling capacity ratio.
  62023        \note This curve is used when a single performance curve does not accurately describe
  62024        \note cooling capacity ratio as a function of temperature.
  62025        \note If a single performance curve is used, leave this field blank.
  62026   A6 , \field Cooling Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of Low Temperature Curve Name
  62027        \type object-list
  62028        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62029        \note Enter a curve name that represents cooling energy ratio as a function of
  62030        \note outdoor dry-bulb temperature and indoor wet-bulb temperature
  62031   A7 , \field Cooling Energy Input Ratio Boundary Curve Name
  62032        \type object-list
  62033        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62034        \note This curve object is used to allow separate low and high cooling energy input ratio
  62035        \note performance curves. This curve represents a line passing through the points where
  62036        \note performance changes. The curve calculates outdoor dry-bulb temperature given weighted
  62037        \note average indoor wet-bulb temperature. If a single performance curve is used,
  62038        \note leave this field blank.
  62039   A8 , \field Cooling Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of High Temperature Curve Name
  62040        \type object-list
  62041        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62042        \note This curve object is used to describe the high outdoor temperature
  62043        \note performance curve used to describe cooling energy ratio.
  62044        \note This curve is used when a single performance curve does not accurately describe
  62045        \note cooling energy ratio as a function of temperature
  62046   A9 , \field Cooling Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of Low Part-Load Ratio Curve Name
  62047        \type object-list
  62048        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62049        \note Enter a curve name that represents cooling energy ratio as a function of
  62050        \note part-load ratio for part-load ratios less than or equal to 1.
  62051        \note If this field is left blank, the model assumes energy is proportional to
  62052        \note part-load ratio.
  62053   A10, \field Cooling Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of High Part-Load Ratio Curve Name
  62054        \type object-list
  62055        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62056        \note Enter a curve name that represents cooling energy ratio as a function of
  62057        \note part-load ratio for part-load ratios greater than 1. Part-load ratios
  62058        \note can exceed 1 in variable speed compression systems.
  62059        \note If this field is left blank, the model assumes energy is proportional to
  62060        \note part-load ratio.
  62061   A11, \field Cooling Combination Ratio Correction Factor Curve Name
  62062        \type object-list
  62063        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62064        \note This curve defines how rated capacity changes when the total indoor terminal unit cooling
  62065        \note capacity is greater than the Gross Rated Total Cooling Capacity defined in this object.
  62066        \note If this field is left blank, the model assumes total indoor terminal unit cooling
  62067        \note capacity is equal to the Gross Rated Total Cooling Capacity defined above.
  62068   A12, \field Cooling Part-Load Fraction Correlation Curve Name
  62069        \type object-list
  62070        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62071        \note This curve defines the cycling losses when the heat pump compressor cycles on and off
  62072        \note below the Minimum Heat Pump Part-Load Ratio specified in the field below.
  62073   N5 , \field Gross Rated Heating Capacity
  62074        \type real
  62075        \units W
  62076        \autosizable
  62077        \note Enter the heating capacity in watts at rated conditions or set to autosize.
  62078        \note Heating capacity not accounting for the effect of supply air fan heat
  62079   N6 , \field Rated Heating Capacity Sizing Ratio
  62080        \type real
  62081        \units W/W
  62082        \minimum 1.0
  62083        \default 1.0
  62084        \note If the Gross Rated Heating Capacity is autosized, the heating capacity is sized
  62085        \note to be equal to the cooling capacity multiplied by this sizing ratio. The zone
  62086        \note terminal unit heating coils are also sized using this ratio unless the sizing
  62087        \note ratio input in the ZoneHVAC:TerminalUnit:VariableRefrigerantFlow object is entered.
  62088   N7 , \field Gross Rated Heating COP
  62089        \type real
  62090        \units W/W
  62091        \minimum> 0.0
  62092        \note COP includes compressor and condenser fan electrical energy input
  62093        \note COP does not include supply fan heat or supply fan electrical energy input
  62094        \default 3.4
  62095   N8 , \field Minimum Outdoor Temperature in Heating Mode
  62096        \type real
  62097        \units C
  62098        \default -20.0
  62099        \note Enter the minimum outdoor temperature allowed for heating operation.
  62100   N9 , \field Maximum Outdoor Temperature in Heating Mode
  62101        \type real
  62102        \units C
  62103        \default 16.0
  62104        \note Enter the maximum outdoor temperature allowed for heating operation.
  62105   A13, \field Heating Capacity Ratio Modifier Function of Low Temperature Curve Name
  62106        \type object-list
  62107        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62108        \note Enter a curve name that represents full load heating capacity ratio as a
  62109        \note function of outdoor wet-bulb temperature and indoor dry-bulb temperature.
  62110        \note Outdoor dry-bulb temperature may be used if wet-bulb temperature data is unavailable.
  62111        \note See Heating Performance Curve Outdoor Temperature Type input below to determine which
  62112        \note outdoor temperature type to use.
  62113        \note Up to two curves are allowed if the performance cannot be represented by a single curve.
  62114        \note The following two fields are used if two curves are required.
  62115   A14, \field Heating Capacity Ratio Boundary Curve Name
  62116        \type object-list
  62117        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62118        \note This curve object is used to allow separate low and high heating capacity ratio
  62119        \note performance curves. This curve represents a line passing through the points where
  62120        \note performance changes. The curve calculates outdoor dry-bulb or wet-bulb temperature
  62121        \note given weighted average indoor dry-bulb temperature. See Heating Performance Curve
  62122        \note Outdoor Temperature Type input below to determine which outdoor temperature type to use.
  62123        \note If a single performance curve is used, leave this field blank.
  62124   A15, \field Heating Capacity Ratio Modifier Function of High Temperature Curve Name
  62125        \type object-list
  62126        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62127        \note This curve object is used to describe the high outdoor temperature
  62128        \note performance curve used to describe heating capacity ratio.
  62129        \note This curve is used when a single performance curve does not accurately describe
  62130        \note heating capacity ratio as a function of temperature.
  62131        \note If a single performance curve is used, leave this field blank.
  62132   A16, \field Heating Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of Low Temperature Curve Name
  62133        \type object-list
  62134        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62135        \note Enter a curve name that represents heating energy ratio as a function of
  62136        \note outdoor wet-bulb temperature and indoor dry-bulb temperature
  62137        \note Outdoor dry-bulb temperature may be used if wet-bulb temperature data is unavailable.
  62138        \note See Heating Performance Curve Outdoor Temperature Type input below to determine which
  62139        \note outdoor temperature type to use.
  62140   A17, \field Heating Energy Input Ratio Boundary Curve Name
  62141        \type object-list
  62142        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62143        \note This curve object is used to allow separate low and high heating energy input ratio
  62144        \note performance curves. This curve represents a line passing through the points where
  62145        \note performance changes. The curve calculates outdoor dry-bulb or wet-bulb temperature
  62146        \note given weighted average indoor dry-bulb temperature. See Heating Performance Curve
  62147        \note Outdoor Temperature Type input below to determine which outdoor temperature type to use.
  62148        \note If a single performance curve is used, leave this field blank.
  62149   A18, \field Heating Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of High Temperature Curve Name
  62150        \type object-list
  62151        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62152        \note This curve object is used to allow separate performance curves for heating energy.
  62153        \note If a single performance curve is used, leave this field blank.
  62154   A19, \field Heating Performance Curve Outdoor Temperature Type
  62155        \type choice
  62156        \key DryBulbTemperature
  62157        \key WetBulbTemperature
  62158        \default WetBulbTemperature
  62159        \note Determines temperature type for heating capacity curves and heating energy curves.
  62160        \note This input determines whether the outdoor air dry-bulb or wet-bulb temperature
  62161        \note is used to evaluate these curves.
  62162   A20, \field Heating Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of Low Part-Load Ratio Curve Name
  62163        \type object-list
  62164        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62165        \note This curve represents the heating energy input ratio for part-load ratios less than 1.
  62166   A21, \field Heating Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of High Part-Load Ratio Curve Name
  62167        \type object-list
  62168        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62169        \note This curve represents the heating energy input ratio for part-load ratios greater than 1.
  62170   A22, \field Heating Combination Ratio Correction Factor Curve Name
  62171        \type object-list
  62172        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62173        \note This curve defines how rated capacity changes when the total indoor terminal unit heating
  62174        \note capacity is greater than the Gross Rated Heating Capacity defined in this object.
  62175        \note If this field is left blank, the model assumes total indoor terminal unit heating
  62176        \note capacity is equal to the Gross Rated Heating Capacity defined above.
  62177   A23, \field Heating Part-Load Fraction Correlation Curve Name
  62178        \type object-list
  62179        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62180        \note This curve defines the cycling losses when the heat pump compressor cycles on and off
  62181        \note below the Minimum Heat Pump Part-Load Ratio specified in the following field.
  62182   N10, \field Minimum Heat Pump Part-Load Ratio
  62183        \type real
  62184        \units dimensionless
  62185        \note Enter the minimum heat pump part-load ratio (PLR). When the cooling or heating PLR is
  62186        \note below this value, the heat pump compressor will cycle to meet the cooling or heating
  62187        \note demand.
  62188        \default 0.15
  62189   A24, \field Zone Name for Master Thermostat Location
  62190        \type object-list
  62191        \object-list ZoneNames
  62192        \note Enter the name of the zone where the master thermostat is located.
  62193   A25, \field Master Thermostat Priority Control Type
  62194        \type choice
  62195        \key LoadPriority
  62196        \key ZonePriority
  62197        \key ThermostatOffsetPriority
  62198        \key MasterThermostatPriority
  62199        \key Scheduled
  62200        \default MasterThermostatPriority
  62201        \note Choose a thermostat control logic scheme. If these control types fail to control zone
  62202        \note temperature within a reasonable limit, consider using multiple VRF systems.
  62203        \note This field is not used when all terminal units are set point controlled.
  62204   A26, \field Thermostat Priority Schedule Name
  62205        \type object-list
  62206        \object-list ScheduleNames
  62207        \note this field is required if Master Thermostat Priority Control Type is Scheduled.
  62208        \note Schedule values of 0 denote cooling, 1 for heating, and all other values disable the system.
  62209   A27, \field Zone Terminal Unit List Name
  62210        \required-field
  62211        \type object-list
  62212        \object-list ZoneTerminalUnitListNames
  62213        \note Enter the name of a ZoneTerminalUnitList. This list connects zone terminal units to this
  62214        \note heat pump.
  62215   A28, \field Heat Pump Waste Heat Recovery
  62216        \type choice
  62217        \key No
  62218        \key Yes
  62219        \default No
  62220        \note This field enables heat recovery operation within this VRF outdoor unit.
  62221   N11, \field Equivalent Piping Length used for Piping Correction Factor in Cooling Mode
  62222        \type real
  62223        \units m
  62224        \note Enter the equivalent length of the farthest terminal unit from the condenser
  62225   N12, \field Vertical Height used for Piping Correction Factor
  62226        \type real
  62227        \units m
  62228        \note Enter the height difference between the highest and lowest terminal unit
  62229   A29, \field Piping Correction Factor for Length in Cooling Mode Curve Name
  62230        \type object-list
  62231        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62232        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62233        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*L^3 + a4*H
  62234        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*CR + a4*CR^2 + a5*(L)(CR)
  62235        \note where L = length and CR = combination ratio
  62236        \note specifies coefficients for a0, a1, a2, and a3 in the PCF equation
  62237   N13, \field Piping Correction Factor for Height in Cooling Mode Coefficient
  62238        \type real
  62239        \units 1/m
  62240        \default 0
  62241        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*L^3 + a4*H
  62242        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*CR + a4*CR^2 + a5*(L)(CR) + a6*H
  62243        \note where L = length, H = height, and CR = combination ratio
  62244        \note specifies coefficient a4 (or a6 for biquadratic) in the PCF equation
  62245   N14, \field Equivalent Piping Length used for Piping Correction Factor in Heating Mode
  62246        \type real
  62247        \units m
  62248        \note Enter the equivalent length of the farthest terminal unit from the condenser
  62249   A30, \field Piping Correction Factor for Length in Heating Mode Curve Name
  62250        \type object-list
  62251        \object-list UnivariateFunctions
  62252        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62253        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*L^3 + a4*H
  62254        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*CR + a4*CR^2 + a5*(L)(CR) + a6*H
  62255        \note where L = length and CR = combination ratio
  62256        \note specifies coefficients for a0, a1, a2, and a3 (or a0-a5 for biquadratic) in the PCF equation
  62257   N15, \field Piping Correction Factor for Height in Heating Mode Coefficient
  62258        \type real
  62259        \units 1/m
  62260        \default 0
  62261        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*L^3 + a4*H
  62262        \note PCF = a0 + a1*L + a2*L^2 + a3*CR + a4*CR^2 + a5*(L)(CR) + a6*H
  62263        \note where L = length, H = height, and CR = combination ratio
  62264        \note specifies coefficient a4 (or a6 for biquadratic) in the PCF equation
  62265   N16, \field Crankcase Heater Power per Compressor
  62266        \type real
  62267        \units W
  62268        \default 33.0
  62269        \note Enter the value of the resistive heater located in the compressor(s). This heater
  62270        \note is used to warm the refrigerant and oil when the compressor is off.
  62271   N17, \field Number of Compressors
  62272        \type integer
  62273        \units dimensionless
  62274        \default 2
  62275        \note Enter the total number of compressor. This input is used only for crankcase
  62276        \note heater calculations.
  62277   N18, \field Ratio of Compressor Size to Total Compressor Capacity
  62278        \type real
  62279        \units W/W
  62280        \default 0.5
  62281        \note Enter the ratio of the first stage compressor to total compressor capacity.
  62282        \note All other compressors are assumed to be equally sized. This inputs is used
  62283        \note only for crankcase heater calculations.
  62284   N19, \field Maximum Outdoor Dry-Bulb Temperature for Crankcase Heater
  62285        \type real
  62286        \units C
  62287        \default 5.0
  62288        \note Enter the maximum outdoor temperature above which the crankcase heaters are disabled.
  62289   A31, \field Defrost Strategy
  62290        \type choice
  62291        \key ReverseCycle
  62292        \key Resistive
  62293        \default Resistive
  62294        \note Select a defrost strategy. Reverse cycle reverses the operating mode from heating to cooling
  62295        \note to melt frost formation on the condenser coil. The resistive strategy uses a resistive heater
  62296        \note to melt the frost.
  62297   A32, \field Defrost Control
  62298        \type choice
  62299        \key Timed
  62300        \key OnDemand
  62301        \default Timed
  62302        \note Choose a defrost control type. Either use a fixed Timed defrost period or select
  62303        \note OnDemand to defrost only when necessary.
  62304   A33, \field Defrost Energy Input Ratio Modifier Function of Temperature Curve Name
  62305        \type object-list
  62306        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62307        \note A valid performance curve must be used if reversecycle defrost strategy is selected.
  62308   N20, \field Defrost Time Period Fraction
  62309        \type real
  62310        \units dimensionless
  62311        \minimum 0.0
  62312        \default 0.058333
  62313        \note Fraction of time in defrost mode.
  62314        \note Only applicable if timed defrost control is specified.
  62315   N21, \field Resistive Defrost Heater Capacity
  62316        \type real
  62317        \units W
  62318        \minimum 0.0
  62319        \default 0.0
  62320        \autosizable
  62321        \note Enter the size of the resistive defrost heating element.
  62322        \note Only applicable if resistive defrost strategy is specified
  62323        \ip-units W
  62324   N22, \field Maximum Outdoor Dry-bulb Temperature for Defrost Operation
  62325        \type real
  62326        \units C
  62327        \default 5.0
  62328        \note Enter the maximum outdoor temperature above which defrost operation is disabled.
  62329   A34, \field Condenser Type
  62330        \type choice
  62331        \key AirCooled
  62332        \key EvaporativelyCooled
  62333        \key WaterCooled
  62334        \default AirCooled
  62335        \note Select either an air-cooled, evaporatively-cooled or water-cooled condenser.
  62336   A35, \field Condenser Inlet Node Name
  62337        \type node
  62338        \note Choose an outside air node name or leave this field blank to use weather data.
  62339        \note If this field is blank, the Condenser Type is assumed to be AirCooled.
  62340        \note This input must be specified if Condenser Type = WaterCooled.
  62341   A36, \field Condenser Outlet Node Name
  62342        \type node
  62343        \note Enter a water outlet node name if Condenser Type = WaterCooled.
  62344        \note Leave this field blank if Condenser Type = Air or EvaporativelyCooled.
  62345   N23, \field Water Condenser Volume Flow Rate
  62346        \type real
  62347        \units m3/s
  62348        \ip-units gal/min
  62349        \autosizable
  62350        \note Only used when Condenser Type = WaterCooled.
  62351   N24, \field Evaporative Condenser Effectiveness
  62352        \type real
  62353        \units dimensionless
  62354        \minimum 0.0
  62355        \maximum 1.0
  62356        \default 0.9
  62357        \note Enter the effectiveness of the evaporatively cooled condenser.
  62358        \note This field is only used when the Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled.
  62359   N25, \field Evaporative Condenser Air Flow Rate
  62360        \type real
  62361        \units m3/s
  62362        \minimum> 0.0
  62363        \autosizable
  62364        \note Used to calculate evaporative condenser water use.
  62365        \note This field is only used when the Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled.
  62366   N26, \field Evaporative Condenser Pump Rated Power Consumption
  62367        \type real
  62368        \units W
  62369        \minimum 0.0
  62370        \default 0.0
  62371        \autosizable
  62372        \note Rated power consumed by the evaporative condenser's water pump.
  62373        \note This field is only used when the Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled.
  62374   A37, \field Supply Water Storage Tank Name
  62375        \type object-list
  62376        \object-list WaterStorageTankNames
  62377        \note A separate storage tank may be used to supply an evaporatively cooled condenser.
  62378   N27, \field Basin Heater Capacity
  62379        \type real
  62380        \units W/K
  62381        \minimum 0.0
  62382        \default 0.0
  62383        \note This field is only used for Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled and for periods
  62384        \note when the basin heater is available (field Basin Heater Operating Schedule Name).
  62385        \note For this situation, the heater maintains the basin water temperature at the basin heater
  62386        \note setpoint temperature when the outdoor air temperature falls below the setpoint temperature.
  62387        \note The basin heater only operates when the DX coil is off.
  62388   N28, \field Basin Heater Setpoint Temperature
  62389        \type real
  62390        \units C
  62391        \minimum 2.0
  62392        \default 2.0
  62393        \note This field is only used for Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled.
  62394        \note Enter the outdoor dry-bulb temperature when the basin heater turns on.
  62395   A38, \field Basin Heater Operating Schedule Name
  62396        \type object-list
  62397        \object-list ScheduleNames
  62398        \note This field is only used for Condenser Type = EvaporativelyCooled.
  62399        \note Schedule values greater than 0 allow the basin heater to operate whenever the outdoor
  62400        \note air dry-bulb temperature is below the basin heater setpoint temperature.
  62401        \note If a schedule name is not entered, the basin heater is allowed to operate
  62402        \note throughout the entire simulation.
  62403   A39, \field Fuel Type
  62404        \type choice
  62405        \key Electricity
  62406        \key NaturalGas
  62407        \key Propane
  62408        \key Diesel
  62409        \key Gasoline
  62410        \key FuelOilNo1
  62411        \key FuelOilNo2
  62412        \key OtherFuel1
  62413        \key OtherFuel2
  62414        \default Electricity
  62415   N29, \field Minimum Outdoor Temperature in Heat Recovery Mode
  62416        \type real
  62417        \units C
  62418        \note The minimum outdoor temperature below which heat
  62419        \note recovery mode will not operate.
  62420   N30, \field Maximum Outdoor Temperature in Heat Recovery Mode
  62421        \type real
  62422        \units C
  62423        \note The maximum outdoor temperature above which heat
  62424        \note recovery mode will not operate.
  62425   A40, \field Heat Recovery Cooling Capacity Modifier Curve Name
  62426        \type object-list
  62427        \object-list BivariateFunctions
  62428        \note Enter the name of a performance curve which represents
  62429        \note the change in cooling capacity when heat recovery is active
  62430        \note A default constant of 0.9 is used if this input is blank
  62431   N31, \field Initial Heat Recovery Cooling Capacity Fraction
  62432        \type real
  62433        \units W/W
  62434        \default 0.5
